","references":[{"file":"Firsts and MOSTS.pdf","page":19,"proof":"Design of Fallingwater by Frank Lloyd Wright","verificationUrl":"https://fallingwater.org/media-resources/fallingwater-facts/"}]},{"id":"TIME43-008","nodeId":"TIME43-008","anchor":"PDF35-FACT-072","subject":"Completion of the Fallingwater guest house","value":"1939","year":1939,"relation":"milestone year","group":"architectural chronology","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","references":[{"file":"Firsts and MOSTS.pdf","page":19,"proof":"Completion of the Fallingwater guest house","verificationUrl":"https://fallingwater.org/media-resources/fallingwater-facts/"}]},{"id":"TIME43-009","nodeId":"TIME43-009","anchor":"PDF35-FACT-083","subject":"Completion of the Palazzetto dello Sport in Rome","value":"1957","year":1957,"relation":"milestone year","group":"architectural chronology","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","references":[{"file":"Firsts and MOSTS.pdf","page":39,"proof":"Completion of the Palazzetto dello Sport in Rome","verificationUrl":"https://www.laboratorionervi.polimi.it/en/portfolio/palazzetto-dello-sport-roma-1956-57/"}]},{"id":"TIME43-010","nodeId":"TIME43-010","anchor":"PDF35-FACT-075","subject":"Winning competition design for San Cataldo Cemetery","value":"1971","year":1971,"relation":"milestone year","group":"architectural chronology","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","references":[{"file":"Firsts and MOSTS.pdf","page":25,"proof":"Winning competition design for San Cataldo Cemetery","verificationUrl":"https://www.fondazionealdorossi.org/opere/1970-1979-3/cimitero-di-san-cataldo-modena-1971-1978-2/"}]},{"id":"TIME43-011","nodeId":"TIME43-011","anchor":"PDF35-FACT-076","subject":"Opening of the Centre Pompidou","value":"1977","year":1977,"relation":"milestone year","group":"architectural chronology","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","references":[{"file":"Firsts and MOSTS.pdf","page":27,"proof":"Opening of the Centre Pompidou","verificationUrl":"https://www.centrepompidou.fr/en/collection/histoire-du-musee-national-dart-moderne-centre-de-creation-industrielle"}]},{"id":"TIME43-012","nodeId":"TIME43-012","anchor":"PDF35-FACT-084","subject":"Completion of the Burj Khalifa","value":"2010","year":2010,"relation":"milestone year","group":"architectural chronology","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","references":[{"file":"Firsts and MOSTS.pdf","page":40,"proof":"Completion of the Burj Khalifa","verificationUrl":"https://www.som.com/projects/burj-khalifa/"}]},{"id":"TIME43-013","nodeId":"TIME43-013","anchor":"PDF35-FACT-080","subject":"Completion of the Heydar Aliyev Center","value":"2012","year":2012,"relation":"milestone year","group":"architectural chronology","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","references":[{"file":"Firsts and MOSTS.pdf","page":35,"proof":"Completion of the Heydar Aliyev Center","verificationUrl":"https://heydar-aliyev-foundation.org/en/content/index/142/"}]},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-001","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-001","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q004","subject":"Which Gothic element carries vault thrust across an aisle to an external pier?","value":"Flying buttress","group":"Gothic","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Gothic","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/gothic-art"}],"explanation74":"They transfer lateral thrust away from the high nave walls, helping permit larger windows."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-002","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-002","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q487","subject":"Which combination characterizes the structural system of a mature Gothic cathedral?","value":"Rib vaults and flying buttresses","group":"Gothic","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Gothic","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/gothic-art"}],"explanation74":"Ribs and buttresses concentrate and redirect structural forces; modern steel systems are anachronistic."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-003","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-003","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q487","subject":"The choir rebuilt for Abbot Suger at Saint-Denis is a landmark of which style?","value":"Early Gothic","group":"Gothic","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Gothic","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/gothic-art"}],"explanation74":"The choir consecrated in 1144 is a key early Gothic work; Rayonnant and Flamboyant are later phases."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-004","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-004","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q220","subject":"What medium is especially central to the international importance of Ravenna's fifth- and sixth-century church interiors?","value":"Mosaic","group":"Ravenna","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ravenna","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/788/"}],"explanation74":"Ravenna preserves major late-antique and early Byzantine mosaics; the other options are different image-making media."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-005","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-005","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Which of these Ravenna monuments stands in Classe?","value":"Sant'Apollinare","group":"Ravenna","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ravenna","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/788/"}],"explanation74":"Sant'Apollinare in Classe stands near Ravenna's ancient port; the other monuments are in Ravenna itself."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-006","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-006","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q142","subject":"Which event ended the Byzantine Empire in 1453?","value":"Ottoman conquest of Constantinople","group":"Byzantine","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Byzantine","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/byzantium-ca-330-1453"}],"explanation74":"Constantinople fell to the Ottomans in 1453; the other events belong to earlier periods."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-007","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-007","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q142","subject":"The Byzantine Empire continued the institutions of which earlier state?","value":"Roman Empire","group":"Byzantine","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Byzantine","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/byzantium-ca-330-1453"}],"explanation74":"The Byzantine Empire developed from the eastern Roman Empire; 'Byzantine' is a later historical label."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-008","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-008","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q151","subject":"In Antonello’s Palermo Virgin Annunciate, which participant is outside the painted scene?","value":"Gabriel","group":"Antonello","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Antonello","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www2.regione.sicilia.it/beniculturali/dirbenicult/info/beniinamovibili/PaAnnunziata.html"}],"explanation74":"The Annunciation is evoked without showing Gabriel; Mary addresses an implied presence beyond the panel."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-009","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-009","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q476","subject":"Which material gives papier collé its name?","value":"Pasted paper","group":"Cubism","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cubism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/cubism"}],"explanation74":"Papier collé uses pasted paper, a technique associated with Synthetic Cubist experiments."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-010","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-010","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q200","subject":"Which practice is least consistent with Cubism?","value":"Single-point illusionism","group":"Cubism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cubism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/cubism"}],"explanation74":"Cubist experiments challenged unified Renaissance perspective; collage and fragmentation were important methods."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-011","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-011","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q056","subject":"The Impressionists’ first independent exhibition in 1874 offered an alternative to which institution?","value":"Paris Salon","group":"Impressionism","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Impressionism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/impressionism-art-and-modernity"}],"explanation74":"The official Paris Salon dominated exhibition opportunities; the other organizations were founded later."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-012","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-012","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q056","subject":"Monet’s repeated views of one subject primarily explore changes in what?","value":"Light","group":"Impressionism","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Impressionism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/impressionism-art-and-modernity"}],"explanation74":"Impressionist practice often emphasizes light, atmosphere and perception rather than a subject's fixed outline."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-013","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-013","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q056","subject":"Which feature is least characteristic of Impressionism?","value":"Academic contour","group":"Impressionism","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Impressionism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/impressionism-art-and-modernity"}],"explanation74":"Impressionist brushwork often loosens the academic emphasis on sharply bounded forms."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-014","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-014","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q097","subject":"A portrait uses intense green and violet on a face without trying to imitate its natural skin colour. Which movement most directly supports this use of colour?","value":"Fauvism","group":"Fauvism","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Fauvism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/fauvism"}],"explanation74":"Fauvist colour could function expressively and independently of naturalistic description."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-015","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-015","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q771","subject":"Which intellectual field was especially influential on Surrealist interest in dreams and the unconscious?","value":"Psychoanalysis","group":"Surrealism","major":"Art History","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Surrealism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/surrealism"}],"explanation74":"Surrealists drew on psychoanalytic ideas about dreams and unconscious mental processes."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-016","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-016","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q771","subject":"Surrealist automatism primarily sought to suspend what?","value":"Conscious control","group":"Surrealism","major":"Art History","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Surrealism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/surrealism"}],"explanation74":"Automatism aimed to allow associations and marks to emerge with reduced conscious direction."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-017","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-017","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q220","subject":"Which eighteenth-century tendency was especially encouraged by archaeological interest in antiquity?","value":"Neoclassicism","group":"Neoclassicism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Neoclassicism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/neoclassicism"}],"explanation74":"Neoclassicism renewed engagement with classical antiquity, including archaeological discoveries and scholarship."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-018","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-018","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q361","subject":"In Romantic aesthetics, overwhelming nature that inspires awe and fear exemplifies what?","value":"The sublime","group":"Romanticism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Romanticism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/romanticism"}],"explanation74":"The sublime involves experiences of magnitude or power that may exceed calm, rational control."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-019","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-019","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q017","subject":"Which treatise helped disseminate Palladio’s architectural ideas?","value":"The Four Books of Architecture","group":"Palladio","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Palladio","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/712/"}],"explanation74":"Palladio published I quattro libri dell’architettura in 1570; the similarly titled ten-book treatise is associated with Vitruvius."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-020","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-020","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q352","subject":"A symmetrical country house with a classical temple front is most directly associated with which tradition?","value":"Palladianism","group":"Palladio","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Palladio","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/712/"}],"explanation74":"A temple-like portico and balanced composition are characteristic of many Palladian villas."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-021","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-021","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q108","subject":"Which school sought to unite art, craft and modern industrial design?","value":"Bauhaus","group":"Bauhaus","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bauhaus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/729/"}],"explanation74":"The Bauhaus linked multiple design disciplines and reconsidered their relation to production."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-022","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-022","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q108","subject":"Which city sequence follows the main locations of the Bauhaus school?","value":"Weimar → Dessau → Berlin","group":"Bauhaus","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bauhaus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/729/"}],"explanation74":"The school began in Weimar, moved to Dessau, and operated briefly in Berlin before closing in 1933."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-023","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-023","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q108","subject":"Which city did not host the Bauhaus school?","value":"Munich","group":"Bauhaus","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Bauhaus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/729/"}],"explanation74":"The school operated in Weimar, Dessau and Berlin. Munich was not one of its locations."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-024","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-024","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q127","subject":"The Iron Bridge in Shropshire is a landmark in the architectural use of which material?","value":"Cast iron","group":"Ironbridge","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ironbridge","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/371/"}],"explanation74":"The bridge's cast-iron structure is a major monument of early industrial engineering."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-025","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-025","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q767","subject":"Saltaire was planned around which industry?","value":"Textiles","group":"Saltaire","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Saltaire","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1028/"}],"explanation74":"Titus Salt’s textile mill and workers’ village illustrate nineteenth-century industrial planning."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-026","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-026","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q770","subject":"The Marshall Plan chiefly supported which postwar objective?","value":"Economic recovery","group":"Marshall","major":"History & Politics","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Marshall","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/marshall-plan"}],"explanation74":"The European Recovery Program supported postwar reconstruction; it did not create the euro."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-027","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-027","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q043","subject":"Which crisis prompted the Berlin Airlift?","value":"Berlin blockade","group":"Berlin","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Berlin","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift"}],"explanation74":"The airlift supplied West Berlin during the 1948–49 blockade; the Wall was a later crisis."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-028","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-028","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q043","subject":"Which sequence of early Cold War developments is chronological?","value":"Marshall speech → Berlin blockade → NATO","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cold War","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato"}],"explanation74":"The speech was in 1947, the blockade began in 1948, and NATO was founded in 1949."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-029","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-029","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q500","subject":"Which industries were pooled under the Schuman proposal?","value":"Coal and steel","group":"Schuman","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Schuman","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/history-eu/1945-59/schuman-declaration-may-1950_en"}],"explanation74":"Coal and steel were essential to both postwar reconstruction and armaments production."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-030","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-030","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q394","subject":"Which country was not one of the six founding members of the European Coal and Steel Community?","value":"United Kingdom","group":"Schuman","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Schuman","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/history-eu/1945-59/schuman-declaration-may-1950_en"}],"explanation74":"The six were France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-031","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-031","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q368","subject":"Pienza’s Renaissance rebuilding is associated with which cultural movement?","value":"Humanism","group":"Pienza","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Pienza","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/789/"}],"explanation74":"Pius II's rebuilding of Pienza made humanist urban ideas visible in a real town."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-032","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-032","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q010","subject":"Pienza Cathedral’s interior draws particularly on which northern European building type?","value":"Hall church","group":"Pienza","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Pienza","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/789/"}],"explanation74":"The building demonstrates coexistence of traditions rather than a clean stylistic break."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-033","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-033","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q628","subject":"Biagio Rossetti's urban work is most closely associated with the Renaissance expansion of:","value":"Ferrara","group":"Ferrara","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ferrara","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/733/"}],"explanation74":"Rossetti helped shape Ferrara's planned expansion under the Este rulers."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-034","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-034","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q554","subject":"Renaissance Urbino flourished under which kind of patronage?","value":"Ducal court","group":"Urbino","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Urbino","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/828/"}],"explanation74":"Urbino shows how courtly patronage could produce cultural influence beyond a city's physical size."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-035","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-035","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q074","subject":"Boccioni’s Unique Forms of Continuity in Space belongs to which movement?","value":"Futurism","group":"Futurism","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Futurism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/81179"}],"explanation74":"The figure's expanding contours express motion and the body's relationship to space."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-036","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-036","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q074","subject":"Which newspaper published Marinetti’s Futurist manifesto in 1909?","value":"Le Figaro","group":"Futurism","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Futurism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/interactives/exhibitions/2009/futurism/"}],"explanation74":"The Paris newspaper Le Figaro carried the manifesto on its front page in February 1909."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-037","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-037","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q052","subject":"Duchamp’s designation of a manufactured object as art introduced which category?","value":"Readymade","group":"Readymade","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Readymade","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/readymade"}],"explanation74":"The readymade shifts attention from manual fabrication to selection, context and designation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-038","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-038","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q205","subject":"Which movement is particularly associated with humble and unconventional materials?","value":"Arte Povera","group":"Arte Povera","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Arte Povera","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/arte-povera"}],"explanation74":"Arte Povera is not simply a label for pictures of poor people; materials and processes are central."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-039","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-039","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q354","subject":"Which formal vocabulary is most characteristic of De Stijl?","value":"Orthogonals and primary colours","group":"De Stijl","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: De Stijl","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/de-stijl"}],"explanation74":"De Stijl promoted an abstract visual language associated with straight lines and elementary colours."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-040","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-040","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q718","subject":"The First Folio is a major collected edition of whose plays?","value":"William Shakespeare","group":"Shakespeare","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Shakespeare","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.folger.edu/explore/shakespeare-in-print/first-folio/"}],"explanation74":"Published in 1623, it preserved eighteen plays not previously printed, including Macbeth."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-041","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-041","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q718","subject":"Shakespeare’s First Folio appeared in which year?","value":"1623","group":"Shakespeare","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Shakespeare","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.folger.edu/explore/shakespeare-in-print/first-folio/"}],"explanation74":"The collection appeared in 1623, seven years after Shakespeare’s death in 1616."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-042","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-042","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q374","subject":"Magna Carta was first issued in which century?","value":"Thirteenth","group":"Magna Carta","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Magna Carta","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.parliament.uk/magnacarta/"}],"explanation74":"The 1215 charter limited aspects of royal power; it did not establish universal suffrage."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-043","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-043","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q318","subject":"The original 1215 Magna Carta is associated with which English king?","value":"John","group":"Magna Carta","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Magna Carta","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.parliament.uk/magnacarta/"}],"explanation74":"King John issued the charter under pressure from rebel barons."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-044","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-044","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q299","subject":"Who shared the 1945 medicine Nobel Prize with Fleming and Chain for work on penicillin?","value":"Howard Florey","group":"Penicillin","major":"Science & Tech","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Penicillin","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1945/florey/biographical/"}],"explanation74":"Florey and Chain helped turn Fleming’s antibacterial discovery into a practical medicine."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-045","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-045","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q438","subject":"Polonium was named in honour of which country?","value":"Poland","group":"Curie","major":"Science & Tech","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Curie","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nrc.gov/regulations-legislation/fact-sheets-brochures/backgrounder-on-polonium-210"}],"explanation74":"The name connects a scientific discovery with Marie Curie's Polish origins."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-046","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-046","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q076","subject":"Le Corbusier’s international body of work is a major contribution to which movement?","value":"Modern Movement","group":"Le Corbusier","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Le Corbusier","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1321/"}],"explanation74":"The property brings together works in different countries as evidence of an international architectural contribution."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-047","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-047","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q076","subject":"The Capitol Complex at Chandigarh is associated with which architect's international work?","value":"Le Corbusier","group":"Le Corbusier","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Le Corbusier","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1321/"}],"explanation74":"Chandigarh is a major setting for Le Corbusier's modern civic architecture in India."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-048","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-048","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q178","subject":"Géricault’s Raft of the Medusa belongs to which movement?","value":"Romanticism","group":"Romanticism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Raft of the Medusa","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa"}],"explanation74":"Géricault turned a contemporary disaster into a major Romantic painting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-049","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-049","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q382","subject":"Antonello’s Virgin Annunciate belongs to which Palermo collection?","value":"Palazzo Abatellis","group":"Antonello","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Antonello","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www2.regione.sicilia.it/beniculturali/dirbenicult/info/beniinamovibili/PaAnnunziata.html"}],"explanation74":"Palazzo Abatellis holds the Palermo panel; the Bellomo holds a different Annunciation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-050","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-050","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q721","subject":"Papier collé is especially associated with which phase of Cubism?","value":"Synthetic","group":"Cubism","major":"Art History","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cubism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/cubism"}],"explanation74":"Synthetic Cubism increasingly used assembled signs and pasted materials rather than only fragmented painted facets."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-051","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-051","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q264","subject":"Which building type makes Saltaire a planned community rather than simply a factory complex?","value":"Workers’ housing","group":"Saltaire","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Saltaire","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1028/"}],"explanation74":"Model settlements addressed workers' living conditions as well as industrial production."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-052","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-052","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q109","subject":"Which organization established a system of collective defence in 1949?","value":"NATO","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cold War","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/nato"}],"explanation74":"NATO was founded in 1949; the Marshall Plan primarily addressed economic recovery."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-053","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-053","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q043","subject":"The Berlin Airlift supplied which part of the city?","value":"Western sectors","group":"Berlin","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Berlin","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://history.state.gov/milestones/1945-1952/berlin-airlift"}],"explanation74":"The airlift sustained the Western sectors and demonstrated resolve during an early Cold War confrontation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-054","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-054","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q520","subject":"The Iron Bridge is a landmark of which historical process?","value":"Industrial Revolution","group":"Ironbridge","major":"Architecture","era":"Contemporary","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ironbridge","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/371/"}],"explanation74":"The material links the bridge to industrial production and changing engineering possibilities."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-055","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-055","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q771","subject":"Which method is least associated with Surrealist practice?","value":"Academic idealization","group":"Surrealism","major":"Art History","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Surrealism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/surrealism"}],"explanation74":"Surrealism sought realities beyond ordinary rational description rather than neutral documentation alone."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-056","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-056","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"The nickname “Fauves” literally means what?","value":"Wild beasts","group":"Fauvism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Fauvism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/fauvism"}],"explanation74":"The nickname responded to the apparently untamed intensity of their colour."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-057","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-057","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q394","subject":"Which organization was founded before the ECSC?","value":"United Nations","group":"Schuman","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Schuman","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/history-eu/1945-59/schuman-declaration-may-1950_en"}],"explanation74":"The UN was founded in 1945; the ECSC treaty was signed in 1951 and entered into force in 1952."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-058","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-058","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q628","subject":"Palladianism in eighteenth-century Britain drew chiefly on which architectural heritage?","value":"Venetian Renaissance","group":"Palladio","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Palladio","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/712/"}],"explanation74":"British Palladianism adapted Palladio’s classical principles from the Renaissance Veneto."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-059","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-059","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q220","subject":"Ravenna’s fifth- and sixth-century monuments chiefly connect which two traditions?","value":"Late Roman and Byzantine","group":"Ravenna","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ravenna","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/788/"}],"explanation74":"Their dates and decoration illuminate major transitions in late antiquity."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-060","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-060","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Which printmaking process uses a burin to cut grooves directly into a metal plate?","value":"Engraving","group":"Printmaking","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Printmaking","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/sites/default/files/migrate_images/content/dam/ngaweb/research/publications/pdfs/gemini-recent-prints-and-sculpture.pdf"}],"explanation74":"Engraving cuts the metal mechanically with a burin, unlike etching's chemical action."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-061","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-061","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q128","subject":"A printmaker wants broad tonal areas resembling a wash rather than only a network of lines. Which intaglio process is especially suitable?","value":"Aquatint","group":"Printmaking","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Printmaking","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/exhibitions/aquatint-its-origins-goya"}],"explanation74":"Aquatint creates tonal areas and is frequently combined with etched lines."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-062","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-062","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q658","subject":"In intaglio printing, the image is printed from which part of the matrix?","value":"Recessed areas","group":"Printmaking","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Printmaking","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/sites/default/files/migrate_images/content/dam/ngaweb/research/publications/pdfs/gemini-recent-prints-and-sculpture.pdf"}],"explanation74":"Intaglio prints from recessed areas, unlike relief printing from raised surfaces."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-063","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-063","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q330","subject":"Which visual tradition influenced Mary Cassatt’s colour prints?","value":"Japanese woodblock prints","group":"Cassatt","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Cassatt","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nga.gov/stories/articles/mary-cassatt-daring-printmaker"}],"explanation74":"Japanese prints offered important models of composition and colour for Cassatt's printmaking."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-064","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-064","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q381","subject":"Which ruler commissioned Castel del Monte?","value":"Frederick II of Hohenstaufen","group":"Castel del Monte","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Castel del Monte","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/398/"}],"explanation74":"The castle belongs to Frederick II's thirteenth-century cultural and political setting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-065","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-065","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q137","subject":"What geometric form recurs in Castel del Monte's principal plan and corner towers?","value":"Octagon","group":"Castel del Monte","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Castel del Monte","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/398/"}],"explanation74":"Its regular octagonal organization is a defining feature; this is more specific than calling it a medieval castle."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-066","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-066","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q008","subject":"Which Italian site is particularly associated with the history of the industrial company town?","value":"Crespi d'Adda","group":"Crespi","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Crespi","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/730/"}],"explanation74":"Crespi d'Adda combines industrial production and a planned residential community for workers."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-067","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-067","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Which settlement was planned as an industrial model village?","value":"Saltaire","group":"Pienza","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Pienza","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/789/"},{"file":"Editorial reference: Pienza","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1028/"}],"explanation74":"The two places address urban organization in very different historical and social contexts."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-068","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-068","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q052","subject":"Henri Rousseau is particularly associated with which category?","value":"Naïve art","group":"Rousseau","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Rousseau","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/henri-rousseau-surprised"}],"explanation74":"Rousseau’s self-taught approach is associated with naïve art; Matisse is a leading Fauvist."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-069","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-069","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q185","subject":"Antonello’s Annunciation of 1474 was made for a church in which town?","value":"Palazzolo Acreide","group":"Antonello","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Antonello","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://catalogo.cultura.gov.it/approfondimento/il-tardo-barocco-nella-sicilia-sud-orientale/il-tardo-barocco-palazzo-acreide/palazzolo-acreide-chiesa-annunziata"}],"explanation74":"The commission was for the Annunziata church at Palazzolo Acreide; the painting is now in Siracusa."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-070","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-070","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q355","subject":"Which tradition particularly informs the detailed interiors in Antonello’s Saint Jerome in his Study?","value":"Netherlandish painting","group":"Antonello","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Antonello","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/antonello-da-messina-saint-jerome-in-his-study"}],"explanation74":"The National Gallery connects its architectural setting and detailed oil technique with Netherlandish painting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-071","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-071","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q070","subject":"Which practice designed Milan’s Bosco Verticale?","value":"Boeri Studio","group":"Contemporary Milan","major":"Architecture","era":"Contemporary","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Contemporary Milan","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.stefanoboeriarchitetti.net/project/bosco-verticale/"}],"explanation74":"The project is credited to Boeri Studio: Stefano Boeri, Gianandrea Barreca and Giovanni La Varra."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-072","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-072","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q001","subject":"Which ruling family commissioned Ferrara’s Addizione Erculea?","value":"Este","group":"Ferrara","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ferrara","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/733/"}],"explanation74":"The expansion was commissioned by Ercole I d’Este and planned by Biagio Rossetti."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-073","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-073","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q700","subject":"In which Italian region is Urbino located?","value":"Marche","group":"Urbino","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Urbino","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/828/"}],"explanation74":"Urbino is a hill town in the Marche, an important geographical anchor for its Renaissance court culture."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-074","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-074","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q027","subject":"Which initiative was announced on 9 May 1950?","value":"Schuman Declaration","group":"Schuman","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Schuman","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/history-eu/1945-59/schuman-declaration-may-1950_en"}],"explanation74":"The date of the Schuman Declaration is commemorated as Europe Day."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-075","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-075","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q620","subject":"Which three categories structure the title and organization of Shakespeare's First Folio?","value":"Comedies, histories and tragedies","group":"Shakespeare","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Shakespeare","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.folger.edu/explore/shakespeare-in-print/first-folio/"}],"explanation74":"The First Folio organizes plays into these three dramatic categories."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-076","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-076","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q279","subject":"The original Magna Carta primarily limited the authority of which institution?","value":"Monarchy","group":"Magna Carta","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Magna Carta","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.parliament.uk/magnacarta/"}],"explanation74":"Constitutional history involves successive developments; a medieval charter should not be equated with modern suffrage."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-077","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-077","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q321","subject":"In a mosaic, the small individual pieces forming the image are called:","value":"Tesserae","group":"Ravenna","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Ravenna","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/708074"}],"explanation74":"Tesserae are the units of a mosaic; voussoirs are wedge-shaped elements of an arch."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-078","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-078","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q004","subject":"Which term denotes a gallery or passage above the nave arcade and below the clerestory?","value":"Triforium","group":"Gothic","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Gothic","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/gothic-art"}],"explanation74":"The triforium occupies the intermediate zone of the nave elevation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-079","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-079","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q161","subject":"What is the principal use of the two Bosco Verticale towers?","value":"Housing","group":"Contemporary Milan","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Contemporary Milan","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.stefanoboeriarchitetti.net/project/bosco-verticale/"}],"explanation74":"These are residential towers with extensive planted balconies, rather than office towers."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-080","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-080","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q738","subject":"Which pope initiated the transformation of his birthplace into Pienza?","value":"Pius II","group":"Pienza","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Pienza","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/789/"}],"explanation74":"Pius II Piccolomini's patronage reshaped the town around its Renaissance centre."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-081","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-081","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q447","subject":"Which city is especially associated with Palladio's urban buildings, including the Basilica Palladiana?","value":"Vicenza","group":"Palladio","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Palladio","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/712/"}],"explanation74":"Vicenza and the Palladian villas of the Veneto form a UNESCO World Heritage property."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-082","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-082","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q459","subject":"Which artist's lifetime is 1475–1564?","value":"Michelangelo","group":"Michelangelo","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Michelangelo","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michelangelo"}],"explanation74":"Michelangelo's long career bridges the High Renaissance and later sixteenth-century developments."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-083","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-083","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q125","subject":"Which architect lived from 1599 to 1667?","value":"Francesco Borromini","group":"Borromini","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Francesco Borromini","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Borromini"}],"explanation74":"Borromini lived from 1599 to 1667. Bernini, another leading Roman Baroque architect, lived from 1598 to 1680."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-084","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-084","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q416","subject":"Which painter's life extended from 1840 to 1926?","value":"Claude Monet","group":"Monet","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Claude Monet","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Claude%20Monet"}],"explanation74":"Monet lived well into the twentieth century; the dates of a movement do not define every member's lifetime."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-085","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-085","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q388","subject":"Which painter belongs to the earliest generation?","value":"Giotto","group":"Artists","major":"Art History","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Giotto","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giotto"}],"explanation74":"Giotto’s career precedes the fifteenth-century painters and the High Renaissance."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-086","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-086","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q173","subject":"Which architect belongs to the earliest generation?","value":"Filippo Brunelleschi","group":"Architects","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Filippo Brunelleschi","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filippo_Brunelleschi"}],"explanation74":"Brunelleschi's career precedes Palladio and the Baroque and eighteenth-century architects."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-087","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-087","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q081","subject":"Which treaty came earliest?","value":"Verdun","group":"Treaties","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Treaty of Verdun","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Verdun"}],"explanation74":"Verdun dates to 843; Tordesillas to 1494, Westphalia to 1648, Utrecht to 1713 and Versailles to 1919."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-088","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-088","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q446","subject":"Which event preceded the French Revolution?","value":"US independence recognized","group":"Revolutions","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Treaty of Paris recognises US independence","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Paris_(1783)"}],"explanation74":"The Treaty of Paris recognized US independence in 1783, before the Revolution began in 1789."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-089","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-089","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q118","subject":"Which congress reorganized Europe after the Napoleonic Wars?","value":"Vienna","group":"Vienna","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Congress of Vienna","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congress_of_Vienna"}],"explanation74":"The Congress of Vienna sought a post-Napoleonic European settlement."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-090","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-090","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q446","subject":"Which sequence of European political events is chronological?","value":"French Revolution → Vienna → Versailles","group":"Modern Europe","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: French Revolution begins","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Revolution"}],"explanation74":"The sequence is 1789, 1814–15, then 1919."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-091","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-091","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q765","subject":"Gutenberg’s European movable-type printing developed in which century?","value":"Fifteenth","group":"Printing","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Gutenberg's movable-type printing in Europe","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Gutenberg"}],"explanation74":"Gutenberg's work belongs to the mid-fifteenth century, before Luther's Reformation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-092","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-092","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q765","subject":"The publication of Copernicus's On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres belongs to which century?","value":"Sixteenth","group":"Science","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_revolutionibus_orbium_coelestium"}],"explanation74":"The work was published in 1543, a key date in the history of astronomy."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-093","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-093","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q494","subject":"Galileo’s trial in 1633 took place during which war?","value":"Thirty Years’ War","group":"Galileo","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Trial of Galileo","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galileo_affair"}],"explanation74":"The Thirty Years’ War lasted from 1618 to 1648; the other wars listed belong to the eighteenth century."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-094","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-094","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q172","subject":"Which painter was still alive in 1970?","value":"Pablo Picasso","group":"Picasso","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Pablo Picasso","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/artists/4609"}],"explanation74":"Picasso died in 1973; Matisse in 1954, Braque in 1963, Léger in 1955 and Mondrian in 1944."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-095","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-095","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q459","subject":"Which artist was already alive when Constantinople fell in 1453?","value":"Leonardo da Vinci","group":"Renaissance","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Leonardo da Vinci","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/artists/leonardo-da-vinci"}],"explanation74":"Leonardo was born in 1452. The others were born after 1453."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-096","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-096","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q223","subject":"Which writer lived through the Black Death that reached Europe in 1347?","value":"Petrarch","group":"Black Death","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Petrarch","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrarch"}],"explanation74":"Petrarch lived from 1304 to 1374; Dante died in 1321 and the others were born much later."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-097","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-097","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q537","subject":"Dante’s lifetime overlapped that of which writer?","value":"Petrarch","group":"Dante","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Dante Alighieri","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dante_Alighieri"}],"explanation74":"Dante lived 1265–1321 and Petrarch 1304–1374; the other writers were born after Dante’s death."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-098","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-098","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q272","subject":"Bosco Verticale forms part of which Milan redevelopment area?","value":"Porta Nuova","group":"Contemporary Milan","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Contemporary Milan","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.stefanoboeriarchitetti.net/project/bosco-verticale/"}],"explanation74":"The towers stand in the Porta Nuova–Isola area; CityLife is a separate redevelopment."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-099","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-099","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q043","subject":"Which event occurred immediately before German reunification in 1990?","value":"Berlin Wall opened","group":"Europe","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: German reunification","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=German%20reunification"}],"explanation74":"The opening of the Berlin Wall in 1989 preceded reunification in 1990; the other events belong to earlier decades or centuries."},{"id":"EDITORIAL74-100","nodeId":"EDITORIAL74-100","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q074","subject":"Which sequence of modern movements and institutions is chronological?","value":"Futurism → Dada → Bauhaus → Surrealism","group":"Modern art","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Modern art","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/interactives/exhibitions/2009/futurism/"},{"file":"Editorial reference: Modern art","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/729/"},{"file":"Editorial reference: Modern art","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/essays/surrealism"},{"file":"Editorial reference: Modern art","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/terms/dada"}],"explanation74":"The reference dates are 1909, 1916, 1919 and 1924 respectively."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-101","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-101","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q252","subject":"Which city led the Delian League?","value":"Athens","group":"Classical Greece","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Delian League founded","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delian_League"}],"explanation74":"The league began as an Athenian-led alliance against Persia."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-102","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-102","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q374","subject":"Which two cities headed the opposing alliances in the Peloponnesian War?","value":"Athens and Sparta","group":"Classical Greece","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Peloponnesian War","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peloponnesian_War"}],"explanation74":"Athens and Sparta led rival coalitions in the war of 431–404 BCE."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-103","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-103","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Who wrote the History of the Peloponnesian War?","value":"Thucydides","group":"Classical Greece","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: History of the Peloponnesian War","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Peloponnesian_War"}],"explanation74":"Thucydides wrote about a war in which he had himself participated."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-104","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-104","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q432","subject":"At which battle did Hannibal inflict a major defeat on Rome in 216 BCE?","value":"Cannae","group":"Punic Wars","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Battle of Cannae","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Cannae"}],"explanation74":"Cannae was a Carthaginian victory; Zama later ended Hannibal’s campaign against Rome."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-105","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-105","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q432","subject":"Who defeated Hannibal at Zama?","value":"Scipio Africanus","group":"Punic Wars","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Battle of Zama","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Zama"}],"explanation74":"Scipio’s victory in 202 BCE was decisive in the Second Punic War."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-106","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-106","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q183","subject":"Henri Matisse’s bold colour at the Salon d’Automne of 1905 is associated with which movement?","value":"Fauvism","group":"Matisse","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Matisse","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/artists/henri-matisse"}],"explanation74":"Matisse was a leading Fauvist. Naïve art is instead associated particularly with self-taught painters such as Henri Rousseau."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-107","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-107","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q479","subject":"Which emperor issued the Constitutio Antoniniana?","value":"Caracalla","group":"Roman citizenship","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Constitutio Antoniniana","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutio_Antoniniana"}],"explanation74":"The measure of 212 extended Roman citizenship to most free inhabitants of the empire."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-108","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-108","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q142","subject":"Which system of imperial government did Diocletian establish?","value":"Tetrarchy","group":"Late Roman Empire","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Tetrarchy established","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetrarchy"}],"explanation74":"The tetrarchy distributed imperial responsibilities among two senior and two junior rulers."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-109","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-109","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q222","subject":"The agreement on religious toleration associated with Constantine and Licinius is named after which city?","value":"Milan","group":"Christianity and empire","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Edict of Milan","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edict_of_Milan"}],"explanation74":"The Edict of Milan dates to 313; it should not be confused with the later establishment of Nicene Christianity."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-110","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-110","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q266","subject":"The first Council of Nicaea met in which year?","value":"325","group":"Christian councils","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: First Council of Nicaea","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Council_of_Nicaea"}],"explanation74":"Nicaea in 325 addressed the Arian controversy; 313 is the date associated with Milan."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-111","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-111","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q142","subject":"Which emperor is associated with the Edict of Thessalonica of 380?","value":"Theodosius I","group":"Christianity and empire","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Edict of Thessalonica","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edict_of_Thessalonica"}],"explanation74":"The edict endorsed Nicene Christianity, unlike the earlier agreement on toleration."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-112","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-112","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q081","subject":"Which treaty divided the Carolingian Empire into three kingdoms?","value":"Verdun","group":"Carolingian Europe","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Treaty of Verdun","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Verdun"}],"explanation74":"Verdun in 843 divided the empire among the sons of Louis the Pious."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-113","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-113","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q602","subject":"Otto I defeated which people at Lechfeld?","value":"Magyars","group":"Ottonian Europe","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Battle of Lechfeld","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Lechfeld"}],"explanation74":"The victory of 955 checked Magyar raids into western Europe."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-114","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-114","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q279","subject":"Which dynasty began its rule with Hugh Capet in 987?","value":"Capetian","group":"French monarchy","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Hugh Capet becomes king","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Capet"}],"explanation74":"The Capetians succeeded the Carolingian line in West Francia."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-115","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-115","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q534","subject":"Who was defeated by William at Hastings in 1066?","value":"Harold II","group":"Norman conquest","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Battle of Hastings and Norman conquest","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Hastings"}],"explanation74":"Harold II was the English king defeated at Hastings; Hardrada had fought at Stamford Bridge."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-116","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-116","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q694","subject":"The Investiture Controversy chiefly concerned the appointment of whom?","value":"Bishops","group":"Medieval church","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Investiture Controversy","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Investiture_Controversy"}],"explanation74":"Pope and emperor disputed authority over appointments to ecclesiastical office."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-117","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-117","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q479","subject":"Which emperor sought absolution at Canossa in 1077?","value":"Henry IV","group":"Medieval church","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Walk to Canossa","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Road_to_Canossa"}],"explanation74":"Henry IV approached Pope Gregory VII during the Investiture Controversy."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-118","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-118","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q482","subject":"The conventional date 1054 is associated with which division?","value":"East–West Schism","group":"Medieval church","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: East–West Schism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East%E2%80%93West_Schism"}],"explanation74":"1054 conventionally marks the estrangement of the Latin and Greek churches; the Western Schism began in 1378."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-119","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-119","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q308","subject":"Where did the papal court reside during most of 1309–1377?","value":"Avignon","group":"Medieval papacy","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Avignon Papacy","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avignon_Papacy"}],"explanation74":"The Avignon Papacy preceded the Western Schism and its rival claimants."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-120","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-120","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q223","subject":"The major European wave of the Black Death began in which century?","value":"Fourteenth","group":"Medieval crisis","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Black Death in Europe","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Death"}],"explanation74":"The European outbreak began in 1347, with regional variation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-121","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-121","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q478","subject":"Which city is associated with Gutenberg’s mid-fifteenth-century printing workshop?","value":"Mainz","group":"Printing and society","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Gutenberg's movable-type printing in Europe","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Gutenberg"}],"explanation74":"Gutenberg’s Bible was produced in Mainz; movable type had earlier East Asian precedents."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-122","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-122","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q765","subject":"Who appeared before Charles V at the Diet of Worms?","value":"Martin Luther","group":"Reformation","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Diet of Worms","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diet_of_Worms"}],"explanation74":"Luther appeared in 1521 following the controversy over his writings."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-123","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-123","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q266","subject":"Which council was central to the Catholic response to the Protestant Reformation?","value":"Trent","group":"Catholic reform","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Council of Trent","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Council_of_Trent"}],"explanation74":"Trent met in three phases between 1545 and 1563."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-124","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-124","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q494","subject":"The Peace of Augsburg chiefly regulated coexistence between Catholics and whom?","value":"Lutherans","group":"Confessional Europe","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Peace of Augsburg","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace_of_Augsburg"}],"explanation74":"The settlement of 1555 concerned Catholic and Lutheran imperial estates; it did not recognize every confession."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-125","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-125","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q229","subject":"Which king issued the Edict of Nantes?","value":"Henry IV","group":"French religious wars","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Edict of Nantes","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edict_of_Nantes"}],"explanation74":"Henry IV issued the settlement in 1598; Louis XIV revoked it in 1685."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-126","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-126","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q279","subject":"Who revoked the Edict of Nantes?","value":"Louis XIV","group":"French religious wars","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Revocation of the Edict of Nantes","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edict_of_Fontainebleau"}],"explanation74":"The revocation in 1685 ended the protections granted under Henry IV."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-127","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-127","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q183","subject":"Which painter was nicknamed “Le Douanier”?","value":"Henri Rousseau","group":"Rousseau","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Rousseau","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/artists/henri-rousseau"}],"explanation74":"Rousseau’s nickname refers to his work at a Paris toll station."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-128","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-128","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q494","subject":"The Peace of Utrecht is associated with which war?","value":"Spanish Succession","group":"Early modern diplomacy","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Peace of Utrecht","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peace_of_Utrecht"}],"explanation74":"The settlement of 1713 helped end the War of the Spanish Succession."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-129","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-129","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q514","subject":"Who wrote The Spirit of the Laws?","value":"Montesquieu","group":"Enlightenment","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Spirit of the Laws","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Spirit_of_Law"}],"explanation74":"Montesquieu’s 1748 study examined political systems and the distribution of powers."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-130","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-130","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q048","subject":"The “general will” is particularly associated with which thinker?","value":"Rousseau","group":"Political thought","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Social Contract","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Social_Contract"}],"explanation74":"Rousseau developed the concept in The Social Contract; it is not simply any temporary majority preference."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-131","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-131","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Who published The Wealth of Nations in 1776?","value":"Adam Smith","group":"Economic thought","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Wealth of Nations","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wealth_of_Nations"}],"explanation74":"Smith’s work is a foundational text of classical political economy."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-132","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-132","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q514","subject":"Who was a principal editor of the Encyclopédie?","value":"Denis Diderot","group":"Enlightenment publishing","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Denis Diderot","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denis_Diderot"}],"explanation74":"Diderot’s editorial project disseminated Enlightenment knowledge."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-133","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-133","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q393","subject":"On the Origin of Species explains evolution chiefly through which mechanism?","value":"Natural selection","group":"History of science","major":"Science & Tech","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: On the Origin of Species published","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/On_the_Origin_of_Species"}],"explanation74":"Darwin’s 1859 work set out natural selection; it should not be confused with later political misuse."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-134","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-134","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q211","subject":"The Rosetta Stone was discovered during whose Egyptian expedition?","value":"Napoleon Bonaparte","group":"Archaeology","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Rosetta Stone discovered by French soldiers","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosetta_Stone"}],"explanation74":"French soldiers found it in 1799; Champollion’s decipherment announcement followed in 1822."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-135","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-135","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q740","subject":"Who announced a major breakthrough in deciphering Egyptian hieroglyphs in 1822?","value":"Jean-François Champollion","group":"History of science","major":"Science & Tech","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Rosetta Stone discovered by French soldiers","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosetta_Stone"}],"explanation74":"Champollion’s work used the Rosetta Stone and other evidence."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-136","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-136","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q253","subject":"Which battle is earliest?","value":"Kadesh","group":"Classical antiquity","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Battle of Kadesh","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Kadesh"}],"explanation74":"Kadesh belongs to the thirteenth century BCE; the other battles are later."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-137","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-137","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q109","subject":"The Truman Doctrine was announced in which year?","value":"1947","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Truman Doctrine","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Truman%20Doctrine"}],"explanation74":"The doctrine belongs to the early Cold War, after the Second World War."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-138","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-138","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q109","subject":"Which alliance was founded in 1955?","value":"Warsaw Pact","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Warsaw Pact formed","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nato.int/cps/en/natohq/declassified_138294.htm"}],"explanation74":"The Warsaw Pact formed six years after NATO."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-139","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-139","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q495","subject":"Which event occurred in the same year as the Hungarian uprising?","value":"Suez Crisis","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Hungarian uprising","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Hungarian%20uprising"}],"explanation74":"Both the Hungarian uprising and the Suez Crisis occurred in 1956."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-140","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-140","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q213","subject":"The Prague Spring took place in which year?","value":"1968","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Prague Spring and Warsaw Pact invasion","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Prague%20Spring%20and%20Warsaw%20Pact%20invasion"}],"explanation74":"The reform movement in Czechoslovakia was suppressed by a Warsaw Pact invasion in 1968."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-141","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-141","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q081","subject":"Which treaty was signed in 1992?","value":"Maastricht","group":"European integration","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Maastricht Treaty signed","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/principles-countries-history/history-eu/1990-99_en"}],"explanation74":"Maastricht was signed in 1992; the European Union formally came into being in 1993."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-142","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-142","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q316","subject":"Which technique became especially prominent in Matisse’s late work?","value":"Paper cut-outs","group":"Matisse","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Matisse","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/artists/henri-matisse"}],"explanation74":"Cut paper became a major late medium; it should not be confused with Cubist pasted-paper experiments."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-143","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-143","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q109","subject":"Operation Barbarossa was the German invasion of which country?","value":"Soviet Union","group":"Second World War","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Operation Barbarossa","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Operation%20Barbarossa"}],"explanation74":"The invasion began in 1941, two years after the attack on Poland."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-144","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-144","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q024","subject":"The Normandy landings occurred before which event?","value":"Liberation of Paris","group":"Second World War","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Liberation of Paris","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Liberation%20of%20Paris"}],"explanation74":"The landings of June 1944 preceded the liberation of Paris in August."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-145","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-145","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q109","subject":"The partition accompanying Indian independence occurred in which year?","value":"1947","group":"Decolonisation","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Indian independence and Partition","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Indian%20independence%20and%20Partition"}],"explanation74":"Independence and Partition occurred in 1947; India became a republic in 1950."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-146","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-146","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q639","subject":"The apparently transparent veil of Sanmartino’s Veiled Christ is made of what?","value":"Marble","group":"Neapolitan sculpture","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Neapolitan sculpture","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.museosansevero.it/the-statue/?lang=en"}],"explanation74":"The veil is carved in the same marble as the figure, not real cloth transformed by an alchemical process."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-147","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-147","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q740","subject":"Which mission first landed humans on the Moon?","value":"Apollo 11","group":"History of science","major":"Science & Tech","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Apollo 11 Moon landing","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Apollo%2011%20Moon%20landing"}],"explanation74":"Apollo 11 achieved the first crewed lunar landing in July 1969."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-148","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-148","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q752","subject":"The Anschluss of 1938 concerned the annexation of which country?","value":"Austria","group":"Interwar Europe","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Anschluss: annexation of Austria","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Anschluss%3A%20annexation%20of%20Austria"}],"explanation74":"Germany annexed Austria in 1938; the Munich Agreement concerned the Sudetenland later that year."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-149","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-149","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q026","subject":"The Weimar Constitution belongs to which country?","value":"Germany","group":"Interwar Europe","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Weimar Constitution adopted","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Weimar%20Constitution%20adopted"}],"explanation74":"The constitution established Germany’s parliamentary republic in 1919."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-150","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-150","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q394","subject":"Which organization was founded in 1945?","value":"United Nations","group":"Postwar institutions","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: United Nations founded","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=United%20Nations%20founded"}],"explanation74":"The UN was founded at the close of the Second World War."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-151","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-151","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q083","subject":"Which EU institution is directly elected by citizens?","value":"European Parliament","group":"EU institutions","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: EU institutions","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/institutions-law-budget/institutions-and-bodies/types-institutions-and-bodies_en"}],"explanation74":"Direct elections distinguish Parliament from the other institutions listed."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-152","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-152","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q266","subject":"National ministers meet in which EU institution?","value":"Council of the EU","group":"EU institutions","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: EU institutions","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/institutions-law-budget/institutions-and-bodies/types-institutions-and-bodies_en"}],"explanation74":"The European Council instead brings together heads of state or government."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-153","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-153","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q083","subject":"Which institution normally proposes new EU legislation?","value":"European Commission","group":"EU institutions","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: EU institutions","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/institutions-law-budget/institutions-and-bodies/types-institutions-and-bodies_en"}],"explanation74":"The Commission has the principal right of legislative initiative."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-154","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-154","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Which institution checks how EU funds are accounted for and spent?","value":"Court of Auditors","group":"EU institutions","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: EU institutions","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/institutions-law-budget/institutions-and-bodies/types-institutions-and-bodies_en"}],"explanation74":"The Court of Auditors performs the EU’s external financial audit."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-155","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-155","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q266","subject":"Which institution determines the EU’s broad political priorities?","value":"European Council","group":"EU institutions","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: EU institutions","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://european-union.europa.eu/institutions-law-budget/institutions-and-bodies/types-institutions-and-bodies_en"}],"explanation74":"Heads of state or government meet in the European Council to set direction."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-156","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-156","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q639","subject":"Who commissioned Sanmartino’s Veiled Christ?","value":"Raimondo di Sangro","group":"Neapolitan sculpture","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Neapolitan sculpture","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.museosansevero.it/the-statue/?lang=en"}],"explanation74":"The patron was Raimondo di Sangro, Prince of Sansevero."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-157","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-157","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q195","subject":"Which principal UN organ is seated in The Hague?","value":"International Court of Justice","group":"United Nations","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: United Nations","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.un.org/en/about-us/main-bodies"}],"explanation74":"The Court sits at the Peace Palace in The Hague."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-158","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-158","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q195","subject":"In contentious cases, who can be parties before the International Court of Justice?","value":"States","group":"International justice","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: International justice","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.icj-cij.org/how-the-court-works"}],"explanation74":"The ICJ resolves disputes between states, unlike courts that prosecute individuals."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-159","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-159","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q195","subject":"Which organization has the International Court of Justice as its principal judicial organ?","value":"United Nations","group":"International justice","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: International justice","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.icj-cij.org/how-the-court-works"}],"explanation74":"The ICJ is a UN organ; it is distinct from both European courts and the International Criminal Court."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-160","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-160","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q044","subject":"A broad rise in the general price level is called what?","value":"Inflation","group":"Inflation","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Inflation","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/ecb-and-you/explainers/tell-me-more/html/what_is_inflation.en.html"}],"explanation74":"Inflation concerns prices generally, not merely the price of one product."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-161","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-161","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q044","subject":"With unchanged nominal income, general price increases reduce what?","value":"Purchasing power","group":"Inflation","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Inflation","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/ecb-and-you/explainers/tell-me-more/html/what_is_inflation.en.html"}],"explanation74":"A fixed amount of money buys fewer goods when prices rise."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-162","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-162","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q772","subject":"Which measure values final production within an economy over a given period?","value":"GDP","group":"National accounts","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: National accounts","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/Series/Back-to-Basics/gross-domestic-product-GDP"}],"explanation74":"GDP measures production within the territory, rather than the government’s outstanding debt."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-163","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-163","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q772","subject":"Which GDP measure adjusts for price changes?","value":"Real GDP","group":"National accounts","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: National accounts","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/Series/Back-to-Basics/gross-domestic-product-GDP"}],"explanation74":"Real GDP separates changes in output from changes in prices."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-164","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-164","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q772","subject":"To avoid double counting, GDP chiefly counts which goods?","value":"Final goods","group":"National accounts","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: National accounts","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/Series/Back-to-Basics/gross-domestic-product-GDP"}],"explanation74":"Intermediate inputs are embodied in final output; counting both in full would duplicate value."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-165","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-165","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q772","subject":"GDP divided by population gives which indicator?","value":"GDP per capita","group":"National accounts","major":"Geography & Civics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: National accounts","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/Series/Back-to-Basics/gross-domestic-product-GDP"}],"explanation74":"Per-capita output is an average, not a statement that everyone earns the same income."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-166","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-166","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q616","subject":"The Iliad and Odyssey are traditionally attributed to whom?","value":"Homer","group":"Ancient literature","major":"Language & Literature","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Iliad and Odyssey","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homer"}],"explanation74":"The attribution is traditional; the poems emerged through a complex oral tradition."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-167","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-167","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q616","subject":"Who is associated with the Theogony?","value":"Hesiod","group":"Ancient literature","major":"Language & Literature","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Theogony","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theogony"}],"explanation74":"Hesiod’s Theogony recounts the origins and genealogies of the gods."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-168","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-168","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q145","subject":"Lorenzetti’s Good and Bad Government cycle was painted for which type of setting?","value":"Civic palace","group":"Sienese painting","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Allegory of Good and Bad Government","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Allegory_of_Good_and_Bad_Government"}],"explanation74":"The frescoes occupy Siena’s Palazzo Pubblico and address the consequences of government."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-169","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-169","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q735","subject":"Which element distinguishes a Roman amphitheatre from a conventional semicircular theatre?","value":"Enclosed oval arena","group":"Roman architecture","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Forum, amphitheatre and theatre","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Forum%2C%20amphitheatre%20and%20theatre"}],"explanation74":"An amphitheatre surrounds its arena; a theatre places the audience before a stage."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-170","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-170","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q207","subject":"Which element carries a dome above a square bay through curved triangular surfaces?","value":"Pendentive","group":"Byzantine structures","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Pendentives and a dome over a square","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Pendentives%20and%20a%20dome%20over%20a%20square"}],"explanation74":"Pendentives mediate between the square below and the circular base of a dome."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-171","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-171","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q071","subject":"A figure’s weight resting mainly on one leg is called what?","value":"Contrapposto","group":"Classical sculpture","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Contrapposto and the weight-bearing leg","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Contrapposto%20and%20the%20weight-bearing%20leg"}],"explanation74":"Contrapposto produces opposed shifts of hips and shoulders."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-172","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-172","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q145","subject":"Ambrogio Lorenzetti’s Presentation at the Temple depicts the presentation of whom?","value":"Christ","group":"Sienese painting","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Presentation at the Temple","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.uffizi.it/en/artworks/presentation-at-the-temple"}],"explanation74":"The altarpiece depicts the infant Christ, not the childhood presentation of Mary."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-173","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-173","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q127","subject":"Boullée’s Cenotaph for Newton was primarily what?","value":"Unbuilt project","group":"Architectural projects","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Cenotaph for Newton: project","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89tienne-Louis_Boull%C3%A9e"}],"explanation74":"The 1784 design remained an architectural proposal."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-174","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-174","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q134","subject":"Which facade type is non-load-bearing?","value":"Curtain wall","group":"Modern building systems","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Curtain wall versus load-bearing wall","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Curtain%20wall%20versus%20load-bearing%20wall"}],"explanation74":"A curtain wall encloses a frame but does not support the building’s principal floor loads."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-175","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-175","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q375","subject":"What does “pilotis” refer to in modernist architecture?","value":"Supporting columns","group":"Modern architecture","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Pilotis, free plan and free facade","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Pilotis%2C%20free%20plan%20and%20free%20facade"}],"explanation74":"Pilotis can raise a building above the ground and free the ground plane."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-176","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-176","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q052","subject":"Which movement foregrounded industrial serial form and the viewer’s bodily experience?","value":"Minimalism","group":"Modern sculpture","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Minimalism: serial form and the viewer's body","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Minimalism"}],"explanation74":"Minimalist work often uses repeated geometric units and emphasizes its physical relation to the viewer."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-177","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-177","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q052","subject":"Which approach treats a work’s geographical site as an essential part of it?","value":"Site-specific art","group":"Contemporary art","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Land art and site specificity","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Land%20art%20and%20site%20specificity"}],"explanation74":"Site specificity makes location part of a work’s meaning rather than a neutral display setting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-178","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-178","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q020","subject":"Action painting is particularly associated with which broader movement?","value":"Abstract Expressionism","group":"Modern painting","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Action painting and colour-field painting","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Action%20painting%20and%20colour-field%20painting"}],"explanation74":"Action painting stresses the physical act of painting within Abstract Expressionism."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-179","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-179","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q052","subject":"Advertising and mass-produced consumer imagery are especially associated with which movement?","value":"Pop Art","group":"Modern painting","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Pop Art, reproduction and consumer imagery","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Pop%20Art%2C%20reproduction%20and%20consumer%20imagery"}],"explanation74":"Pop Art reworked imagery from commercial and popular culture."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-180","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-180","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q485","subject":"Which tendency openly reintroduced historical quotation into late twentieth-century architecture?","value":"Postmodernism","group":"Architectural movements","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Postmodern architecture and historical quotation","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Postmodern%20architecture%20and%20historical%20quotation"}],"explanation74":"Postmodern architects used historical references and signs, often challenging strict modernist orthodoxy."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-181","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-181","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q488","subject":"Externally expressed services and structural components are associated with which tendency?","value":"High-tech","group":"Architecture and technology","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: High-tech architecture and expressed services","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=High-tech%20architecture%20and%20expressed%20services"}],"explanation74":"High-tech architecture makes systems and construction visibly part of the design."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-182","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-182","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q276","subject":"Mantegna’s Camera degli Sposi was painted for which family?","value":"Gonzaga","group":"Renaissance patronage","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Camera degli Sposi","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camera_degli_Sposi"}],"explanation74":"The room in Mantua presents the Gonzaga court with illusionistic architectural effects."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-183","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-183","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"The Très Riches Heures was commissioned for which patron?","value":"Duke of Berry","group":"Manuscripts","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Très Riches Heures: Limbourg campaign","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_Duc_de_Berry"}],"explanation74":"The Limbourg brothers worked on the book of hours for Jean, Duke of Berry."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-184","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-184","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q628","subject":"Who designed the Old Sacristy of San Lorenzo?","value":"Brunelleschi","group":"Renaissance architecture","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Old Sacristy of San Lorenzo","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Sacristy"}],"explanation74":"Brunelleschi designed the Old Sacristy; Michelangelo later designed the New Sacristy."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-185","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-185","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q097","subject":"Who painted the Arnolfini Portrait?","value":"Jan van Eyck","group":"Northern Renaissance","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Arnolfini Portrait","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arnolfini_Portrait"}],"explanation74":"Van Eyck’s 1434 painting is oil on oak; the sitters’ identities and the scene’s meaning require care."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-186","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-186","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"Jean Pucelle’s Hours of Jeanne d’Évreux is which kind of object?","value":"Illuminated prayer book","group":"Manuscripts","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Hours of Jeanne d'Évreux","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/470309"}],"explanation74":"It is a small devotional manuscript, not an architectural fresco cycle."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-187","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-187","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q578","subject":"Who painted The Tempest?","value":"Giorgione","group":"Venetian painting","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Tempest","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Tempest_(Giorgione)"}],"explanation74":"Giorgione’s Venetian landscape has a debated subject; no single narrative interpretation should be treated as settled."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-188","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-188","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q298","subject":"Raphael’s School of Athens chiefly represents which intellectual field?","value":"Philosophy","group":"Renaissance iconography","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: School of Athens","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_School_of_Athens"}],"explanation74":"The fresco represents ancient philosophy in the Stanza della Segnatura."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-189","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-189","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q458","subject":"The distorted skull in Holbein’s The Ambassadors demonstrates what?","value":"Anamorphosis","group":"Perspective techniques","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Ambassadors","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ambassadors_(Holbein)"}],"explanation74":"The skull resolves from an oblique viewpoint; this is anamorphic projection."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-190","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-190","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q764","subject":"Which painter’s Last Supper in San Giorgio Maggiore uses a strongly diagonal table?","value":"Tintoretto","group":"Venetian painting","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Last Supper, San Giorgio Maggiore","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last_Supper_(Tintoretto)"}],"explanation74":"Tintoretto’s late work differs from Leonardo’s frontal composition and uses supernatural light."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-191","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-191","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Caravaggio’s Supper at Emmaus in London was painted in which year?","value":"1601","group":"Caravaggio versions","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Supper at Emmaus: London version","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supper_at_Emmaus_(Caravaggio,_London)"}],"explanation74":"The London version dates to 1601; the Milan version dates to 1606."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-192","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-192","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q560","subject":"Donatello’s marble Saint George was commissioned for which Florentine building?","value":"Orsanmichele","group":"Early Renaissance","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Saint George","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_George_(Donatello)"}],"explanation74":"The sculpture was made for an exterior niche of Orsanmichele."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-193","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-193","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q637","subject":"David’s Oath of the Horatii was painted before which event?","value":"French Revolution","group":"Art and revolution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Oath of the Horatii","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oath_of_the_Horatii"}],"explanation74":"The painting dates to 1784, five years before the French Revolution began."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-194","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-194","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q174","subject":"David’s Death of Marat belongs to which historical context?","value":"French Revolution","group":"Art and revolution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Death of Marat","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Death_of_Marat"}],"explanation74":"Painted in 1793, it commemorated a revolutionary figure murdered that year."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-195","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-195","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q643","subject":"Goya’s The Third of May 1808 depicts reprisals by troops of which power?","value":"Napoleonic France","group":"Art and war","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Third of May 1808","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Third_of_May_1808"}],"explanation74":"The painting commemorates French reprisals in Madrid; it was painted in 1814, not 1808."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-196","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-196","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q178","subject":"Which contemporary event prompted Géricault’s Raft of the Medusa?","value":"Shipwreck","group":"Romantic painting","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Raft of the Medusa","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Raft_of_the_Medusa"}],"explanation74":"The 1816 wreck and its political scandal prompted the painting of 1818–1819."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-197","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-197","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q066","subject":"Liberty Leading the People refers to which revolution?","value":"July 1830","group":"Art and revolution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Liberty Leading the People","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty_Leading_the_People"}],"explanation74":"Delacroix responded to the July Revolution of 1830, not the French Revolution of 1789."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-198","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-198","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q577","subject":"The Stone Breakers is associated with which painter?","value":"Gustave Courbet","group":"Realism","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Stone Breakers","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Stone_Breakers"}],"explanation74":"Courbet’s labour scene is a key Realist work; the original was destroyed in 1945."},{"id":"EDITORIAL75-199","nodeId":"EDITORIAL75-199","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q052","subject":"Manet’s Olympia was painted in 1863 and exhibited at the Salon in which year?","value":"1865","group":"Modern art exhibitions","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Olympia","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Olympia"}],"explanation74":"The two dates are distinct: completion in 1863 and its controversial Salon exhibition in 1865."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-200","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-200","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q138","subject":"Which construction principle characterizes Brunelleschi’s Florence Cathedral dome?","value":"Double shell","group":"Florentine dome","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Florence Cathedral: dome","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florence_Cathedral"}],"explanation74":"The dome has inner and outer masonry shells; its lantern was completed later."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-201","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-201","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q721","subject":"Gentile da Fabriano’s 1423 Adoration of the Magi was commissioned by which family?","value":"Strozzi","group":"International Gothic","major":"Art History","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Adoration of the Magi","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adoration_of_the_Magi_(Gentile_da_Fabriano)"}],"explanation74":"The sumptuous Florentine altarpiece was a Strozzi commission."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-202","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-202","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q458","subject":"The skeleton beneath Masaccio’s Holy Trinity serves principally as what?","value":"Memento mori","group":"Renaissance devotion","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Holy Trinity","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Trinity_(Masaccio)"}],"explanation74":"The skeleton reminds the viewer of death within the fresco’s devotional setting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-203","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-203","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q185","subject":"Ghiberti’s Gates of Paradise were made chiefly in which material?","value":"Gilded bronze","group":"Florentine relief","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Gates of Paradise","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gates_of_Paradise"}],"explanation74":"The east doors of the Florence Baptistery contain ten gilded bronze narrative panels."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-204","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-204","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q053","subject":"Which city identifies the Annunciation panel now in the Museo Diocesano, distinct from the San Marco fresco?","value":"Cortona","group":"Fra Angelico","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Annunciation: Cortona version","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annunciation_of_Cortona"}],"explanation74":"Fra Angelico’s Cortona panel is a separate work from his San Marco Annunciation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-205","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-205","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q631","subject":"Van der Weyden’s Descent from the Cross was commissioned by which Leuven guild?","value":"Crossbowmen","group":"Northern Renaissance","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Descent from the Cross","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Descent_from_the_Cross_(van_der_Weyden)"}],"explanation74":"The crossbowmen’s guild commissioned the altarpiece; its small crossbow motifs recall that patronage."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-206","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-206","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q290","subject":"The Battle of San Romano depicted by Uccello occurred in which year?","value":"1432","group":"Uccello","major":"Art History","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Battle of San Romano","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/paolo-uccello-the-battle-of-san-romano"}],"explanation74":"The battle took place in 1432; the three panels were painted later."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-207","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-207","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Donatello’s Gattamelata commemorates which commander?","value":"Erasmo da Narni","group":"Equestrian monuments","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Equestrian statue of Gattamelata","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equestrian_statue_of_Gattamelata"}],"explanation74":"Gattamelata was the nickname of the condottiere Erasmo da Narni."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-208","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-208","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q150","subject":"Michelozzo’s Palazzo Medici was originally built for whom?","value":"Cosimo de’ Medici","group":"Florentine palaces","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Palazzo Medici Riccardi","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palazzo_Medici_Riccardi"}],"explanation74":"Cosimo the Elder commissioned the palace; the Riccardi later extended it."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-209","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-209","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q628","subject":"Filarete’s Ca’ Granda was originally designed as what?","value":"Hospital","group":"Renaissance Milan","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Ospedale Maggiore: foundation","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ospedale_Maggiore_of_Milan"}],"explanation74":"The Ospedale Maggiore was a hospital; later uses should not be confused with its original purpose."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-210","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-210","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q194","subject":"Gozzoli’s Procession of the Magi decorates the chapel of which palace?","value":"Palazzo Medici","group":"Florentine patronage","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Procession of the Magi","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magi_Chapel"}],"explanation74":"The biblical procession incorporates portraits connected with the Medici court."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-211","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-211","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q505","subject":"Piero’s civic fresco of the Resurrection is in which town?","value":"Sansepolcro","group":"Piero della Francesca","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Resurrection","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resurrection_(Piero_della_Francesca)"}],"explanation74":"The Resurrection belongs to Sansepolcro’s civic setting, distinct from his Arezzo fresco cycle."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-212","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-212","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q346","subject":"Who directed construction of Sant’Andrea in Mantua after Alberti’s death?","value":"Luca Fancelli","group":"Alberti","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Sant'Andrea, Mantua: construction begins","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_Sant'Andrea,_Mantua"}],"explanation74":"Fancelli carried forward Alberti’s project, begun in 1472."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-213","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-213","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q664","subject":"Who is portrayed in Leonardo’s Lady with an Ermine?","value":"Cecilia Gallerani","group":"Leonardo portraits","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Lady with an Ermine","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_with_an_Ermine"}],"explanation74":"The sitter was Cecilia Gallerani, connected with Ludovico Sforza’s Milanese court."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-214","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-214","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q756","subject":"What happened to Leonardo’s great Sforza equestrian monument?","value":"Remained an uncast model","group":"Leonardo projects","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Sforza equestrian monument: model","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonardo's_horse"}],"explanation74":"Leonardo completed a clay model, but the intended bronze monument was never cast."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-215","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-215","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q065","subject":"Carpaccio’s Saint Ursula paintings were conceived as what?","value":"Narrative cycle","group":"Venetian confraternities","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Legend of Saint Ursula","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legend_of_Saint_Ursula"}],"explanation74":"The paintings formed a narrative cycle for a Venetian confraternity."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-216","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-216","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q631","subject":"Bosch’s Garden of Earthly Delights is arranged as what?","value":"Triptych","group":"Northern painting","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Garden of Earthly Delights","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.museodelprado.es/en/the-collection/art-work/the-garden-of-earthly-delights-triptych/02388242-6d6a-4e9e-a992-e1311eab3609"}],"explanation74":"The work comprises a central panel and two hinged wings."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-217","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-217","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q053","subject":"A “sacra conversazione” typically presents the Virgin and saints in what kind of setting?","value":"Unified sacred space","group":"Renaissance altarpieces","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: San Zaccaria Altarpiece","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Zaccaria_Altarpiece"}],"explanation74":"Bellini’s San Zaccaria Altarpiece is an example of saints sharing one unified devotional setting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-218","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-218","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q340","subject":"Who commissioned Raphael’s Triumph of Galatea at the Villa Farnesina?","value":"Agostino Chigi","group":"Raphael","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Triumph of Galatea","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Triumph_of_Galatea"}],"explanation74":"The banker Agostino Chigi commissioned decoration for his Roman villa."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-219","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-219","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q053","subject":"The Sistine Madonna was originally made for a church in which city?","value":"Piacenza","group":"Raphael","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Sistine Madonna","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sistine_Madonna"}],"explanation74":"It was an altarpiece for San Sisto in Piacenza, despite its later presence in Dresden."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-220","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-220","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q071","subject":"Michelangelo’s Moses was made for whose tomb?","value":"Julius II","group":"Michelangelo","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Moses","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moses_(Michelangelo)"}],"explanation74":"The Moses belongs to the repeatedly revised project for Julius II’s tomb."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-221","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-221","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q412","subject":"Dürer’s Rhinoceros woodcut was based primarily on what?","value":"Reports and drawings","group":"Dürer","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Rhinoceros","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C3%BCrer's_Rhinoceros"}],"explanation74":"Dürer did not see the animal himself; his image drew on written and visual reports."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-222","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-222","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q459","subject":"The title Madonna of the Harpies refers to figures on which part of the painting?","value":"Pedestal","group":"Andrea del Sarto","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Madonna of the Harpies","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madonna_of_the_Harpies"}],"explanation74":"The unusual creatures decorate the pedestal beneath the Virgin."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-223","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-223","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q054","subject":"Titian’s Bacchus and Ariadne was made for which patron?","value":"Alfonso d’Este","group":"Mythological painting","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Bacchus and Ariadne","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bacchus_and_Ariadne"}],"explanation74":"It formed part of Alfonso d’Este’s camerino decoration in Ferrara."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-224","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-224","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q220","subject":"Which expected object is absent from Pontormo’s Capponi Chapel Deposition?","value":"Cross","group":"Mannerism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Deposition","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deposition_from_the_Cross_(Pontormo)"}],"explanation74":"Despite its conventional title, no cross appears in the composition."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-225","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-225","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q053","subject":"Dürer presented his Four Apostles panels to which city?","value":"Nuremberg","group":"Dürer","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Four Apostles","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Four_Apostles"}],"explanation74":"The panels were a gift to Nuremberg in 1526."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-226","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-226","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q732","subject":"Correggio’s Assumption in Parma Cathedral uses the dome to suggest what?","value":"An opening into heaven","group":"Correggio","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Assumption, Parma Cathedral dome","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assumption_of_the_Virgin_(Correggio)"}],"explanation74":"The illusionistic composition visually dissolves the architectural boundary overhead."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-227","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-227","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q435","subject":"Giulio Romano’s Fall of the Giants occupies a room in which palace?","value":"Palazzo Te","group":"Mannerist interiors","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Fall of the Giants","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sala_dei_Giganti"}],"explanation74":"The frescoes at Palazzo Te blur the boundary between painted architecture and the actual room."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-228","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-228","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q220","subject":"The elongated proportions of Madonna with the Long Neck are associated with which tendency?","value":"Mannerism","group":"Parmigianino","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Madonna with the Long Neck","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madonna_with_the_Long_Neck"}],"explanation74":"Parmigianino’s deliberate elongation exemplifies Mannerist departures from High Renaissance balance."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-229","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-229","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q574","subject":"Cellini’s celebrated gold Salt Cellar was made for which ruler?","value":"Francis I","group":"Decorative arts","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Cellini Salt Cellar","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cellini_Salt_Cellar"}],"explanation74":"Cellini made the table sculpture for Francis I of France."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-230","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-230","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q399","subject":"A Burial at Ornans gives monumental scale to which subject?","value":"Provincial funeral","group":"Courbet","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: A Burial at Ornans","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Burial_At_Ornans"}],"explanation74":"Courbet gave an ordinary provincial ceremony the scale traditionally reserved for prestigious history painting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-231","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-231","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q624","subject":"Millais’s Ophelia draws its subject from which play?","value":"Hamlet","group":"Pre-Raphaelites","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Ophelia","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Ophelia"}],"explanation74":"Ophelia is a character in Hamlet; the painting combines literary narrative with detailed vegetation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-232","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-232","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q577","subject":"Courbet exhibited The Painter’s Studio in 1855 in which setting?","value":"Independent pavilion","group":"Courbet","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Painter's Studio","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Painter%27s%20Studio"}],"explanation74":"Courbet mounted his own Pavilion of Realism alongside the Paris exhibition."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-233","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-233","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"The activity in Millet’s The Gleaners consists of collecting what?","value":"Leftover ears of grain","group":"Millet","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Gleaners","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gleaners"}],"explanation74":"Gleaning was the collection of harvest remnants, giving the scene a distinct social context."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-234","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-234","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q200","subject":"The two rural figures in Millet’s The Angelus are engaged in what?","value":"Prayer","group":"Millet","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Angelus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Angelus_(painting)"}],"explanation74":"The figures pause their agricultural work for prayer."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-235","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-235","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q097","subject":"The Bellelli Family is principally which kind of work?","value":"Family portrait","group":"Degas","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Bellelli Family","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Bellelli%20Family"}],"explanation74":"Degas uses the arrangement of relatives and furniture to convey relationships and emotional distance."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-236","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-236","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Le Déjeuner sur l’herbe was shown in 1863 at which exhibition?","value":"Salon des Refusés","group":"Manet","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Le Déjeuner sur l'herbe","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Le%20D%C3%A9jeuner%20sur%20l%27herbe"}],"explanation74":"The painting was exhibited at the Salon des Refusés, not the 1874 Impressionist exhibition."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-237","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-237","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q576","subject":"Which painter’s 1863 Birth of Venus exemplifies academic mythology rather than Manet’s contemporary nude?","value":"Alexandre Cabanel","group":"Nineteenth-century art","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Birth of Venus","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Birth%20of%20Venus"}],"explanation74":"Cabanel’s idealized mythological nude offers a useful contrast with Manet’s Olympia."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-238","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-238","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q056","subject":"Monet’s Impression, Sunrise depicts the harbour of which city?","value":"Le Havre","group":"Impressionism","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Impression, Sunrise","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Impression%2C%20Sunrise"}],"explanation74":"The work represents Le Havre; Rouen is associated with Monet’s later cathedral series."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-239","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-239","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q416","subject":"Monet’s Woman with a Parasol of 1875 belongs to which phase of his career?","value":"Argenteuil","group":"Monet","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Woman with a Parasol","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Woman%20with%20a%20Parasol"}],"explanation74":"The 1875 painting belongs to Monet’s Argenteuil period, before the later Giverny parasol paintings."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-240","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-240","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q181","subject":"Dance at Le Moulin de la Galette chiefly depicts what?","value":"Parisian leisure","group":"Renoir","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Dance at Le Moulin de la Galette","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Dance%20at%20Le%20Moulin%20de%20la%20Galette"}],"explanation74":"Renoir painted a crowded scene of modern leisure with changing outdoor light."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-241","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-241","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"The Thinker was originally conceived for which larger project?","value":"The Gates of Hell","group":"Rodin","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Thinker","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Thinker"}],"explanation74":"Its initial role within The Gates of Hell preceded later enlargements and independent casts."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-242","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-242","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"How many painted versions of Isle of the Dead did Böcklin produce?","value":"Five","group":"Böcklin","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Isle of the Dead: versions","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Isle%20of%20the%20Dead%3A%20versions"}],"explanation74":"Böcklin painted five versions; dates and collections depend on the particular version."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-243","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-243","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q114","subject":"Which phase of Van Gogh’s career includes The Potato Eaters?","value":"Early Dutch period","group":"Van Gogh","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Potato Eaters","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Potato%20Eaters"}],"explanation74":"The dark-toned rural scene predates his brighter French paintings."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-244","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-244","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q056","subject":"Which label was applied retrospectively to diverse artists rather than one unified group?","value":"Post-Impressionism","group":"Modern movements","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Post-Impressionism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Post-Impressionism"}],"explanation74":"Post-Impressionism groups varied approaches and does not identify a single organization with one manifesto."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-245","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-245","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q524","subject":"Wheatfield with Crows was painted during Van Gogh’s stay in which place?","value":"Auvers-sur-Oise","group":"Van Gogh","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Wheatfield with Crows","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Wheatfield%20with%20Crows"}],"explanation74":"The work belongs to his Auvers period; it is not securely established as his final painting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-246","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-246","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q563","subject":"Sullivan and Adler’s Wainwright Building was principally designed as what?","value":"Office building","group":"American architecture","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Wainwright Building","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Wainwright%20Building"}],"explanation74":"Its composition articulates the function of a tall office building."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-247","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-247","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q190","subject":"Where Do We Come From? What Are We? Where Are We Going? was painted in which setting?","value":"Tahiti","group":"Gauguin","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Where Do We Come From? What Are We? Where Are We Going?","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Where%20Do%20We%20Come%20From%3F%20What%20Are%20We%3F%20Where%20Are%20We%20Going%3F"}],"explanation74":"Gauguin produced the large symbolic painting in Tahiti in 1897–1898."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-248","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-248","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q416","subject":"Which place supplied the garden motif for Monet’s Water Lilies?","value":"Giverny","group":"Monet","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Water Lilies: series","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Water%20Lilies%3A%20series"}],"explanation74":"Monet repeatedly painted the water garden at Giverny over many years."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-249","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-249","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q183","subject":"Which alternative title identifies Matisse’s Harmony in Red?","value":"The Red Room","group":"Matisse","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Harmony in Red","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Harmony%20in%20Red"}],"explanation74":"Harmony in Red is also called The Red Room; The Red Studio is a separate work."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-250","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-250","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q721","subject":"Braque’s Houses at L’Estaque is particularly associated with the emergence of what?","value":"Cubism","group":"Braque","major":"Art History","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Houses at L'Estaque","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Houses%20at%20L%27Estaque"}],"explanation74":"The geometric simplification of this 1908 landscape helped lead towards Cubism."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-251","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-251","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q074","subject":"The repeated paws and leash in Balla’s Dynamism of a Dog on a Leash suggest the influence of what?","value":"Motion photography","group":"Futurism","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Dynamism of a Dog on a Leash","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Dynamism%20of%20a%20Dog%20on%20a%20Leash"}],"explanation74":"Successive positions evoke photographic studies of movement."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-252","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-252","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q708","subject":"Boccioni’s original Unique Forms of Continuity in Space of 1913 was made in what?","value":"Plaster","group":"Futurist sculpture","major":"Art History","era":"Early 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Unique Forms of Continuity in Space","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Unique%20Forms%20of%20Continuity%20in%20Space"}],"explanation74":"The original is plaster; familiar bronze casts were made later."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-253","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-253","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q445","subject":"De Chirico’s empty arcades and long shadows chiefly belong to which movement?","value":"Pittura Metafisica","group":"Metaphysical painting","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Mystery and Melancholy of a Street","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Mystery%20and%20Melancholy%20of%20a%20Street"}],"explanation74":"Metaphysical painting uses familiar urban forms to create an enigmatic atmosphere."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-254","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-254","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q053","subject":"In which city did Dada first emerge in 1916?","value":"Zurich","group":"Dada","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Dada emerges in Zurich","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dada"}],"explanation74":"Dada began in wartime Zurich, later developing distinct centres elsewhere."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-255","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-255","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q359","subject":"Mendelsohn’s Einstein Tower was built for which function?","value":"Astronomical observation","group":"Expressionism","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Einstein Tower","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Einstein%20Tower"}],"explanation74":"The Potsdam tower is an observatory, not a memorial tower without a scientific function."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-256","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-256","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q709","subject":"Who collaborated with Mies van der Rohe on the Barcelona Pavilion?","value":"Lilly Reich","group":"Barcelona Pavilion","major":"Architecture","era":"Early 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Barcelona Pavilion","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Barcelona%20Pavilion"}],"explanation74":"Lilly Reich’s collaboration is part of the pavilion’s design history."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-257","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-257","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q316","subject":"The Treachery of Images principally distinguishes an object from what?","value":"Its representation","group":"Magritte","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Treachery of Images","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=The%20Treachery%20of%20Images"}],"explanation74":"The painted pipe is an image, not a usable pipe."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-258","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-258","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q303","subject":"Woman with Her Throat Cut belongs to Giacometti’s involvement with which movement?","value":"Surrealism","group":"Giacometti","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Woman with Her Throat Cut","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Woman%20with%20Her%20Throat%20Cut"}],"explanation74":"The 1932 sculpture belongs to his Surrealist period, before the best-known elongated postwar figures."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-259","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-259","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q381","subject":"Terragni’s Casa del Fascio was originally commissioned for which organization?","value":"Fascist Party","group":"Italian Rationalism","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Casa del Fascio in Como","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Casa%20del%20Fascio%20in%20Como"}],"explanation74":"Its modernist form does not imply democratic political patronage."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-260","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-260","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q322","subject":"Fallingwater’s projecting terraces prominently use which structural form?","value":"Cantilever","group":"Wright","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Fallingwater","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Fallingwater"}],"explanation74":"Reinforced-concrete cantilevers project over the waterfall setting."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-261","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-261","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q172","subject":"Picasso’s Guernica was made for Spain’s pavilion at which event?","value":"Paris exhibition of 1937","group":"Spanish Civil War","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Guernica","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Guernica"}],"explanation74":"The Spanish Republic exhibited it in Paris after the bombing of Guernica."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-262","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-262","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q486","subject":"Which sculptor created Montserrat for the context of the 1937 Spanish pavilion?","value":"Julio González","group":"Spanish sculpture","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Montserrat","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Montserrat"}],"explanation74":"González’s sculpture is associated with the Spanish Civil War and the Republic’s pavilion."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-263","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-263","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q154","subject":"Le Corbusier’s Marseille Unité d’Habitation is principally which building type?","value":"Collective housing","group":"Postwar housing","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Unité d'Habitation in Marseille completed","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Unit%C3%A9%20d%27Habitation%20in%20Marseille%20completed"}],"explanation74":"The postwar project combines apartments with shared services."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-264","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-264","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q009","subject":"Which element organizes the exhibition route in Wright’s New York Guggenheim?","value":"Spiral ramp","group":"Museum architecture","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum opens","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solomon_R._Guggenheim_Museum"}],"explanation74":"The ramp creates a continuous route around the central void."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-265","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-265","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q084","subject":"The Decameron’s storytellers leave Florence because of what?","value":"Plague","group":"Boccaccio","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Decameron","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Decameron"}],"explanation74":"The framing narrative responds to the plague of 1348."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-266","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-266","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"The Canterbury Tales is framed by which journey?","value":"Pilgrimage","group":"Chaucer","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Canterbury Tales","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Canterbury_Tales"}],"explanation74":"The storytellers travel together as pilgrims."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-267","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-267","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q036","subject":"Petrarch’s epic Africa was written in which language?","value":"Latin","group":"Petrarch","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Africa","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Africa_(Petrarch)"}],"explanation74":"The epic treats Scipio Africanus in Latin, unlike Petrarch’s vernacular lyrics."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-268","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-268","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q616","subject":"Castiglione’s Book of the Courtier chiefly concerns what?","value":"Courtly conduct","group":"Renaissance literature","major":"Language & Literature","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Book of the Courtier","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Courtier"}],"explanation74":"The dialogue explores the accomplishments and behaviour expected of a courtier."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-269","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-269","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q342","subject":"The Prince was first published in 1532, but was chiefly written in which year?","value":"1513","group":"Machiavelli","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: The Prince: publication","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Prince"}],"explanation74":"Composition and publication differ; the book appeared after Machiavelli’s death."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-270","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-270","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q036","subject":"Utopia was first published in which language?","value":"Latin","group":"Thomas More","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Utopia","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utopia_(book)"}],"explanation74":"The first edition of 1516 was in Latin."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-271","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-271","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"The first edition of Orlando furioso appeared in which year?","value":"1516","group":"Ariosto","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Orlando furioso: first edition","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orlando_Furioso"}],"explanation74":"Later editions followed in 1521 and 1532; 1532 is not the first publication."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-272","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-272","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q718","subject":"In Praise of Folly was written in 1509 and first published in which year?","value":"1511","group":"Erasmus","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: In Praise of Folly","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_Praise_of_Folly"}],"explanation74":"The composition date and first publication date are distinct."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-273","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-273","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q307","subject":"Which Roman event occurred in 212 CE?","value":"Citizenship extension","group":"Roman history","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Constitutio Antoniniana","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitutio_Antoniniana"}],"explanation74":"Caracalla’s citizenship measure belongs to 212, before the tetrarchy and Constantine."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-274","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-274","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q478","subject":"Which dynasty ruled Persia during Justinian’s reign?","value":"Sasanian","group":"Late antiquity","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Sasanian Empire","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sasanian_Empire"}],"explanation74":"The Sasanian Empire was the eastern Roman Empire’s major Persian rival in this period."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-275","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-275","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q318","subject":"The sack of Constantinople by the Fourth Crusade occurred in which year?","value":"1204","group":"Medieval trade","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Sack of Constantinople","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sack_of_Constantinople"}],"explanation74":"1204 marks the Latin conquest; Byzantine rule was restored in 1261."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-276","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-276","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q142","subject":"The Western Schism involved rival claims to which office?","value":"Papacy","group":"Avignon and Rome","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Western Schism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Schism"}],"explanation74":"The schism of 1378–1417 involved competing papal obediences, not the East–West division of 1054."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-277","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-277","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q266","subject":"Hugh Capet’s accession conventionally dates to which year?","value":"987","group":"European dynasties","major":"History & Politics","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Hugh Capet becomes king","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Capet"}],"explanation74":"987 marks the start of the Capetian royal line; 962 concerns Otto I’s imperial coronation."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-278","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-278","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q374","subject":"Which came first?","value":"Gutenberg Bible","group":"Printing and reform","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Gutenberg's movable-type printing in Europe","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Gutenberg"}],"explanation74":"The mid-fifteenth-century Bible preceded the sixteenth-century Reformation milestones."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-279","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-279","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q770","subject":"The Soviet Union dissolved in which year?","value":"1991","group":"Cold War","major":"History & Politics","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Dissolution of the Soviet Union","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Dissolution%20of%20the%20Soviet%20Union"}],"explanation74":"The fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989 and German reunification in 1990 preceded the Soviet dissolution."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-280","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-280","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q083","subject":"The European Union formally came into being in which year?","value":"1993","group":"European integration","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: European Union established","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/collection/eu-law/treaties/treaties-founding.html?locale=en"}],"explanation74":"Maastricht was signed in 1992 and entered into force in 1993."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-281","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-281","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q318","subject":"Which year is often called the “Year of Africa”?","value":"1960","group":"Decolonisation","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Year of Africa","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Year%20of%20Africa"}],"explanation74":"Many African states gained independence in 1960."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-282","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-282","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q195","subject":"OPEC was founded in which year?","value":"1960","group":"International economy","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: OPEC founded","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=OPEC%20founded"}],"explanation74":"Its founding predates the 1973 oil crisis."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-283","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-283","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"The Locarno Treaties belong to which decade?","value":"1920s","group":"Interwar diplomacy","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Locarno Treaties","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Locarno%20Treaties"}],"explanation74":"The treaties were concluded in 1925, during attempts at European diplomatic stabilization."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-284","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-284","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q109","subject":"The Kellogg–Briand Pact is associated with which objective?","value":"Renunciation of war","group":"Interwar diplomacy","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Kellogg–Briand Pact","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Kellogg%E2%80%93Briand%20Pact"}],"explanation74":"The 1928 pact sought to renounce war as an instrument of national policy."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-285","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-285","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q027","subject":"Which conference followed Germany’s surrender in May 1945?","value":"Potsdam","group":"Postwar conferences","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Potsdam Conference","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Potsdam%20Conference"}],"explanation74":"Potsdam met in July–August 1945, after Germany’s surrender."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-286","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-286","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q318","subject":"Khrushchev’s Secret Speech was delivered in which year?","value":"1956","group":"Soviet history","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Khrushchev's Secret Speech","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Khrushchev%27s%20Secret%20Speech"}],"explanation74":"The speech criticizing Stalin’s rule was delivered in 1956."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-287","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-287","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q451","subject":"India became a republic in which year?","value":"1950","group":"Modern India","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: India becomes a republic","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=India%20becomes%20a%20republic"}],"explanation74":"Independence and Partition came in 1947; republican status followed in 1950."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-288","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-288","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q451","subject":"The Republic of Turkey was proclaimed in which year?","value":"1923","group":"Modern Turkey","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Republic of Turkey proclaimed","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Republic%20of%20Turkey%20proclaimed"}],"explanation74":"The republic was proclaimed in 1923, after the Ottoman Empire’s dissolution."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-289","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-289","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q195","subject":"Which international agency was established in 1957?","value":"IAEA","group":"Nuclear energy","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: International Atomic Energy Agency established","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=International%20Atomic%20Energy%20Agency%20established"}],"explanation74":"The International Atomic Energy Agency was established in 1957."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-290","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-290","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q213","subject":"Which country experienced the Prague Spring?","value":"Czechoslovakia","group":"Eastern Europe","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Prague Spring and Warsaw Pact invasion","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Prague%20Spring%20and%20Warsaw%20Pact%20invasion"}],"explanation74":"The reform movement developed in Czechoslovakia in 1968."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-291","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-291","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q109","subject":"Which declaration of 1941 set out Roosevelt and Churchill’s common principles?","value":"Atlantic Charter","group":"Second World War","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: Atlantic Charter","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=Atlantic%20Charter"}],"explanation74":"The Atlantic Charter preceded US entry into the war after Pearl Harbor."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-292","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-292","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q318","subject":"De Gaulle’s Appeal of 18 June was delivered in which year?","value":"1940","group":"Modern France","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record: De Gaulle's Appeal of 18 June","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?search=De%20Gaulle%27s%20Appeal%20of%2018%20June"}],"explanation74":"The appeal followed the collapse of French resistance to the German invasion in June 1940."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-293","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-293","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q183","subject":"Matisse studied at the École des Beaux-Arts under which painter?","value":"Gustave Moreau","group":"Matisse","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Matisse","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/artists/henri-matisse"}],"explanation74":"Moreau taught Matisse in the 1890s, before the emergence of Fauvism."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-294","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-294","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q435","subject":"Antonello’s Annunciation now in Palazzo Bellomo was painted in which year?","value":"1474","group":"Antonello","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Antonello","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://catalogo.cultura.gov.it/approfondimento/il-tardo-barocco-nella-sicilia-sud-orientale/il-tardo-barocco-palazzo-acreide/palazzolo-acreide-chiesa-annunziata"}],"explanation74":"The Bellomo painting is dated 1474; the Palermo Virgin Annunciate has a less certain date."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-295","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-295","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q070","subject":"Which feature chiefly accommodates the trees of Bosco Verticale?","value":"Projecting balconies","group":"Contemporary Milan","major":"Architecture","era":"Contemporary","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Contemporary Milan","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.stefanoboeriarchitetti.net/project/bosco-verticale/"}],"explanation74":"Large planted balconies form the characteristic vegetated facades of the towers."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-296","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-296","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q639","subject":"Sanmartino completed the Veiled Christ in which year?","value":"1753","group":"Neapolitan sculpture","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Neapolitan sculpture","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.museosansevero.it/the-statue/?lang=en"}],"explanation74":"The dated sculpture belongs to the eighteenth century, not the high seventeenth-century Roman Baroque."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-297","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-297","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q399","subject":"Which animal is the subject of Rousseau’s Surprised! of 1891?","value":"Tiger","group":"Rousseau","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Rousseau","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/henri-rousseau-surprised"}],"explanation74":"The painting presents a tiger within dense vegetation and stormy weather."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-298","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-298","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q066","subject":"Which Paris exhibition gave the Fauves their critical nickname in 1905?","value":"Salon d’Automne","group":"Early modernism","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Early modernism","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/artists/henri-matisse"}],"explanation74":"The name followed the group’s appearance at the Salon d’Automne of 1905."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-299","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-299","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q382","subject":"Antonello’s Palermo Virgin Annunciate uses which medium and support?","value":"Oil on panel","group":"Antonello","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Antonello","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www2.regione.sicilia.it/beniculturali/dirbenicult/info/beniinamovibili/PaAnnunziata.html"}],"explanation74":"The small painting is oil on wood, not a fresco."},{"id":"EDITORIAL76-300","nodeId":"EDITORIAL76-300","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q332","subject":"Henri Rousseau’s artistic training is best described as what?","value":"Self-taught","group":"Rousseau","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"knowledge check","references":[{"file":"Editorial reference: Rousseau","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/henri-rousseau-surprised"}],"explanation74":"Rousseau developed his painting outside conventional academic training."},{"id":"EXP77-1301","nodeId":"EXP77-1301","anchor":"M77-MOMA-81477","subject":"Horse and Rider","value":"Terracotta","group":"Marino Marini","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/81477"}],"explanation74":"The collection records terracotta, rather than the bronze often associated with Marini’s sculpture."},{"id":"EXP77-1302","nodeId":"EXP77-1302","anchor":"M77-MOMA-81021","subject":"Caryatid","value":"Limestone","group":"Amedeo Modigliani","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/81021"}],"explanation74":"This caryatid is carved in limestone; Modigliani’s sculptural work is distinct from his painted portraits."},{"id":"EXP77-1303","nodeId":"EXP77-1303","anchor":"M77-MOMA-81368","subject":"Crucifixion","value":"Glazing","group":"Lucio Fontana","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/81368"}],"explanation74":"MoMA records the sculpture as glazed ceramic."},{"id":"EXP77-1304","nodeId":"EXP77-1304","anchor":"M77-MOMA-79951","subject":"Sackcloth 1953","value":"Burlap","group":"Alberto Burri","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/79951"}],"explanation74":"Burlap and thread form the work; the material itself becomes central to the image."},{"id":"EXP77-1305","nodeId":"EXP77-1305","anchor":"M77-MOMA-79070","subject":"Full Fathom Five","value":"Nails and buttons","group":"Jackson Pollock","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/79070"}],"explanation74":"Pollock embedded everyday objects, including nails, buttons and coins, in the painted surface."},{"id":"EXP77-1306","nodeId":"EXP77-1306","anchor":"M77-MOMA-78386","subject":"One: Number 31, 1950","value":"Enamel paint","group":"Jackson Pollock","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/78386"}],"explanation74":"MoMA records oil and enamel paint on canvas, consistent with Pollock’s poured-paint practice."},{"id":"EXP77-1307","nodeId":"EXP77-1307","anchor":"M77-MOMA-79419","subject":"Dynamic Hieroglyphic of the Bal Tabarin","value":"Sequins","group":"Gino Severini","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/79419"}],"explanation74":"The collection lists oil on canvas with sequins, linking surface effects to the dance-hall subject."},{"id":"EXP77-1308","nodeId":"EXP77-1308","anchor":"M77-MOMA-79435","subject":"Man with Yellow Pants","value":"Polished stainless steel","group":"Michelangelo Pistoletto","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/79435"}],"explanation74":"The figure is applied to polished stainless steel, allowing the surrounding space to enter the work."},{"id":"EXP77-1309","nodeId":"EXP77-1309","anchor":"M77-MOMA-80539","subject":"Great Metaphysical Interior","value":"Ferrara","group":"Giorgio de Chirico","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/80539"}],"explanation74":"MoMA places its execution in Ferrara in 1917, distinguishing it from his earlier Paris works."},{"id":"EXP77-1310","nodeId":"EXP77-1310","anchor":"M77-MOMA-80620","subject":"Map of the World","value":"Embroidery","group":"Alighiero Boetti","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/80620"}],"explanation74":"The work is embroidered on fabric; its map is constructed through textile labour."},{"id":"EXP77-1311","nodeId":"EXP77-1311","anchor":"M77-MOMA-80955","subject":"Line 1000 Meters Long","value":"An inked paper roll","group":"Piero Manzoni","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/80955"}],"explanation74":"A metal drum contains paper bearing a line along its thousand-metre length."},{"id":"EXP77-1312","nodeId":"EXP77-1312","anchor":"M77-MOMA-80380","subject":"Reservist of the First Division","value":"A thermometer","group":"Kazimir Malevich","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/80380"}],"explanation74":"The mixed-media work incorporates a thermometer along with printed paper and a postage stamp."},{"id":"EXP77-1313","nodeId":"EXP77-1313","anchor":"M77-MOMA-79874","subject":"Spatial Concept: Expectation","value":"Slashing","group":"Lucio Fontana","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/79874"}],"explanation74":"The canvas is cut open; the opening makes real space part of the work."},{"id":"EXP77-1314","nodeId":"EXP77-1314","anchor":"M77-MOMA-78364","subject":"Achrome","value":"Kaolin","group":"Piero Manzoni","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/78364"}],"explanation74":"The collection identifies kaolin on canvas, rather than a conventionally painted image."},{"id":"EXP77-1315","nodeId":"EXP77-1315","anchor":"M77-MOMA-80873","subject":"Places with No Street","value":"Neon tubing","group":"Mario Merz","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","relation":"context","references":[{"file":"Museum collection record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/80873"}],"explanation74":"The collection lists neon tubing alongside stones, twigs and an aluminium structure."},{"id":"EXP77-1316","nodeId":"EXP77-1316","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"The Nightmare","value":"Henry Fuseli","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Nightmare"}],"explanation74":"The Nightmare is associated with Henry Fuseli. Detroit version; first exhibited in 1782."},{"id":"EXP77-1317","nodeId":"EXP77-1317","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q048","subject":"Critique of Pure Reason","value":"Immanuel Kant","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critique_of_Pure_Reason"}],"explanation74":"Critique of Pure Reason is associated with Immanuel Kant. First edition; second revised edition in 1787."},{"id":"EXP77-1318","nodeId":"EXP77-1318","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q048","subject":"The Social Contract","value":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Social_Contract"}],"explanation74":"The Social Contract is associated with Jean-Jacques Rousseau. Political legitimacy and the general will."},{"id":"EXP77-1319","nodeId":"EXP77-1319","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q066","subject":"The Barque of Dante","value":"Eugène Delacroix","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Barque_of_Dante"}],"explanation74":"The Barque of Dante is associated with Eugène Delacroix. Salon debut; subject from Dante's Inferno."},{"id":"EXP77-1320","nodeId":"EXP77-1320","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"The Meeting","value":"Jean-Honoré Fragonard","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.frick.org/blogs/curatorial/mapping_provenance_fragonards_progress_love"}],"explanation74":"The Meeting is associated with Jean-Honoré Fragonard. One of the four original Progress of Love paintings commissioned for Madame du Barry."},{"id":"EXP77-1321","nodeId":"EXP77-1321","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q050","subject":"Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems","value":"Galileo Galilei","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dialogue_Concerning_the_Two_Chief_World_Systems"}],"explanation74":"Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems is associated with Galileo Galilei. Publication preceded Galileo's 1633 trial."},{"id":"EXP77-1322","nodeId":"EXP77-1322","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q128","subject":"Saint Jerome cycle, Scuola di San Giorgio degli Schiavoni","value":"Vittore Carpaccio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scuola_di_San_Giorgio_degli_Schiavoni"}],"explanation74":"Saint Jerome cycle, Scuola di San Giorgio degli Schiavoni is associated with Vittore Carpaccio. The source names Saint Jerome without fixing one scene; retain the Venetian cycle instead of guessing a single painting."},{"id":"EXP77-1323","nodeId":"EXP77-1323","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"The Judgment of Solomon","value":"Nicolas Poussin","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://collections.louvre.fr/ark:/53355/cl010062441"}],"explanation74":"The Judgment of Solomon is associated with Nicolas Poussin. Biblical judgment staged with clear rhetorical gestures."},{"id":"EXP77-1324","nodeId":"EXP77-1324","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"The Swing","value":"Jean-Honoré Fragonard","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Swing_(Fragonard)"}],"explanation74":"The Swing is associated with Jean-Honoré Fragonard. Rococo scene built around concealed viewpoints and social play."},{"id":"EXP77-1325","nodeId":"EXP77-1325","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q583","subject":"In Praise of Folly","value":"Erasmus","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_Praise_of_Folly"}],"explanation74":"In Praise of Folly is associated with Erasmus. Written in 1509; first published 1511."},{"id":"EXP77-1326","nodeId":"EXP77-1326","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q052","subject":"Marie de' Medici cycle","value":"Peter Paul Rubens","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marie_de'_Medici_cycle"}],"explanation74":"Marie de' Medici cycle is associated with Peter Paul Rubens. Royal biography presented through history and allegory."},{"id":"EXP77-1327","nodeId":"EXP77-1327","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q320","subject":"St Paul's Cathedral, London","value":"Christopher Wren","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Paul's_Cathedral"}],"explanation74":"St Paul's Cathedral, London is associated with Christopher Wren. Rebuilt after the Great Fire of 1666."},{"id":"EXP77-1328","nodeId":"EXP77-1328","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q238","subject":"Book of the Courtier","value":"Baldassare Castiglione","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Book_of_the_Courtier"}],"explanation74":"Book of the Courtier is associated with Baldassare Castiglione. Published in 1528; courtly conduct and sprezzatura."},{"id":"EXP77-1329","nodeId":"EXP77-1329","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Conversion on the Way to Damascus","value":"Caravaggio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conversion_on_the_Way_to_Damascus"}],"explanation74":"Conversion on the Way to Damascus is associated with Caravaggio. Second Cerasi Chapel version; distinguish the first commission's panel."},{"id":"EXP77-1330","nodeId":"EXP77-1330","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q097","subject":"Self-Portrait at Twenty-Eight","value":"Albrecht Dürer","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-Portrait_(D%C3%BCrer,_Munich)"}],"explanation74":"Self-Portrait at Twenty-Eight is associated with Albrecht Dürer. Frontal pose and artist's monogram."},{"id":"EXP77-1331","nodeId":"EXP77-1331","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"The Blue Boy","value":"Thomas Gainsborough","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blue_Boy"}],"explanation74":"The Blue Boy is associated with Thomas Gainsborough. Costume portrait; sitter traditionally identified as Jonathan Buttall."},{"id":"EXP77-1332","nodeId":"EXP77-1332","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q240","subject":"Discourse on the Method","value":"René Descartes","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discourse_on_the_Method"}],"explanation74":"Discourse on the Method is associated with René Descartes. Published with scientific essays in French."},{"id":"EXP77-1333","nodeId":"EXP77-1333","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q238","subject":"Novum Organum","value":"Francis Bacon","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novum_Organum"}],"explanation74":"Novum Organum is associated with Francis Bacon. Treatise on method and the reform of knowledge."},{"id":"EXP77-1334","nodeId":"EXP77-1334","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Aphrodite of Knidos","value":"Praxiteles","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aphrodite_of_Knidos"}],"explanation74":"Aphrodite of Knidos is associated with Praxiteles. Lost original; survives through copies and adaptations."},{"id":"EXP77-1335","nodeId":"EXP77-1335","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Crucifixion of Saint Peter","value":"Caravaggio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crucifixion_of_Saint_Peter_(Caravaggio)"}],"explanation74":"Crucifixion of Saint Peter is associated with Caravaggio. Cerasi Chapel, Santa Maria del Popolo, Rome."},{"id":"EXP77-1336","nodeId":"EXP77-1336","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"The Bolt","value":"Jean-Honoré Fragonard","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://collections.louvre.fr/ark:/53355/cl010064766"}],"explanation74":"The Bolt is associated with Jean-Honoré Fragonard. Le Verrou; Louvre painting, distinct from The Meeting and The Swing."},{"id":"EXP77-1337","nodeId":"EXP77-1337","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q145","subject":"Madonna of Vico l'Abate","value":"Ambrogio Lorenzetti","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://edv.seai.uniroma1.it/it/inscriptions/edv08_023.html"}],"explanation74":"Madonna of Vico l'Abate is associated with Ambrogio Lorenzetti. Dated panel for Sant'Angelo at Vico l'Abate; the photo's abbreviated title is resolved by the inscription record."},{"id":"EXP77-1338","nodeId":"EXP77-1338","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q035","subject":"Gargantua and Pantagruel","value":"François Rabelais","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gargantua_and_Pantagruel"}],"explanation74":"Gargantua and Pantagruel is associated with François Rabelais. Publication sequence; the posthumous fifth book's authorship is debated."},{"id":"EXP77-1339","nodeId":"EXP77-1339","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Paradise Lost","value":"John Milton","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Lost"}],"explanation74":"Paradise Lost is associated with John Milton. First edition in ten books; revised to twelve in 1674."},{"id":"EXP77-1340","nodeId":"EXP77-1340","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q145","subject":"Presentation at the Temple","value":"Ambrogio Lorenzetti","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.uffizi.it/en/artworks/presentation-at-the-temple"}],"explanation74":"Presentation at the Temple is associated with Ambrogio Lorenzetti. Signed and dated Sienese altarpiece. The scene is Christ's presentation, not Mary's childhood presentation."},{"id":"EXP77-1341","nodeId":"EXP77-1341","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q732","subject":"Assumption, Parma Cathedral dome","value":"Correggio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assumption_of_the_Virgin_(Correggio)"}],"explanation74":"Assumption, Parma Cathedral dome is associated with Correggio. Illusionistic opening of the dome into heaven."},{"id":"EXP77-1342","nodeId":"EXP77-1342","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q293","subject":"Santi Luca e Martina","value":"Pietro da Cortona","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santi_Luca_e_Martina"}],"explanation74":"Santi Luca e Martina is associated with Pietro da Cortona. Central Roman Baroque church; later finishing work continued."},{"id":"EXP77-1343","nodeId":"EXP77-1343","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q350","subject":"Hours of Jeanne d'Évreux","value":"Jean Pucelle","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/470309"}],"explanation74":"Hours of Jeanne d'Évreux is associated with Jean Pucelle. Illuminated prayer book by Jean Pucelle."},{"id":"EXP77-1344","nodeId":"EXP77-1344","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q113","subject":"Principia Mathematica","value":"Isaac Newton","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophi%C3%A6_Naturalis_Principia_Mathematica"}],"explanation74":"Principia Mathematica is associated with Isaac Newton. Mathematical treatment of motion and gravitation."},{"id":"EXP77-1345","nodeId":"EXP77-1345","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q046","subject":"Les Amours","value":"Pierre de Ronsard","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Amours"}],"explanation74":"Les Amours is associated with Pierre de Ronsard. First collection dedicated to Cassandre; later additions and editions followed."},{"id":"EXP77-1346","nodeId":"EXP77-1346","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q320","subject":"St Peter's Square","value":"Gian Lorenzo Bernini","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Peter's_Square"}],"explanation74":"St Peter's Square is associated with Gian Lorenzo Bernini. Colonnades frame the approach to the basilica."},{"id":"EXP77-1347","nodeId":"EXP77-1347","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Dijon altarpiece wings","value":"Melchior Broederlam","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melchior_Broederlam"}],"explanation74":"Dijon altarpiece wings is associated with Melchior Broederlam. Melchior Broederlam's Annunciation, Visitation, Presentation and Flight into Egypt."},{"id":"EXP77-1348","nodeId":"EXP77-1348","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q260","subject":"Sorrows of Young Werther","value":"Johann Wolfgang von Goethe","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sorrows_of_Young_Werther"}],"explanation74":"Sorrows of Young Werther is associated with Johann Wolfgang von Goethe. Epistolary novel associated with Sturm und Drang."},{"id":"EXP77-1349","nodeId":"EXP77-1349","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q320","subject":"St Peter's Baldachin","value":"Gian Lorenzo Bernini","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Peter's_Baldachin"}],"explanation74":"St Peter's Baldachin is associated with Gian Lorenzo Bernini. Monumental bronze canopy over the papal altar; workshop collaboration."},{"id":"EXP77-1350","nodeId":"EXP77-1350","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q032","subject":"Le Cid","value":"Pierre Corneille","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Cid"}],"explanation74":"Le Cid is associated with Pierre Corneille. Tragicomedy that provoked a major literary controversy."},{"id":"EXP77-1351","nodeId":"EXP77-1351","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Jerusalem Delivered","value":"Torquato Tasso","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerusalem_Delivered"}],"explanation74":"Jerusalem Delivered is associated with Torquato Tasso. Authorised publication of the epic set during the First Crusade."},{"id":"EXP77-1352","nodeId":"EXP77-1352","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Legend of Saint Ursula","value":"Vittore Carpaccio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legend_of_Saint_Ursula"}],"explanation74":"Legend of Saint Ursula is associated with Vittore Carpaccio. Narrative cycle for a Venetian confraternity."},{"id":"EXP77-1353","nodeId":"EXP77-1353","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q009","subject":"Altes Museum","value":"Karl Friedrich Schinkel","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altes_Museum"}],"explanation74":"Altes Museum is associated with Karl Friedrich Schinkel. Berlin museum; public collection presented through classical civic architecture."},{"id":"EXP77-1354","nodeId":"EXP77-1354","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"The Angelus","value":"Jean-François Millet","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Angelus_(painting)"}],"explanation74":"The Angelus is associated with Jean-François Millet. Rural prayer scene; conventional execution range."},{"id":"EXP77-1355","nodeId":"EXP77-1355","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q238","subject":"Dictionary of the English Language","value":"Samuel Johnson","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Dictionary_of_the_English_Language"}],"explanation74":"Dictionary of the English Language is associated with Samuel Johnson. First edition published 1755."},{"id":"EXP77-1356","nodeId":"EXP77-1356","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Farnese Gallery ceiling","value":"Annibale Carracci","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Loves_of_the_Gods"}],"explanation74":"Farnese Gallery ceiling is associated with Annibale Carracci. Roman mythological fresco cycle; Triumph of Bacchus and Ariadne at its centre."},{"id":"EXP77-1357","nodeId":"EXP77-1357","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Tom Jones","value":"Henry Fielding","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_History_of_Tom_Jones,_a_Foundling"}],"explanation74":"Tom Jones is associated with Henry Fielding. Novel first published in 1749."},{"id":"EXP77-1358","nodeId":"EXP77-1358","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q173","subject":"Old Sacristy of San Lorenzo","value":"Filippo Brunelleschi","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Sacristy"}],"explanation74":"Old Sacristy of San Lorenzo is associated with Filippo Brunelleschi. Florence; centralised spatial geometry and Medici patronage."},{"id":"EXP77-1359","nodeId":"EXP77-1359","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q294","subject":"Boy with a Basket of Fruit","value":"Caravaggio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boy_with_a_Basket_of_Fruit"}],"explanation74":"Boy with a Basket of Fruit is associated with Caravaggio. Early Roman work; dating approximate."},{"id":"EXP77-1360","nodeId":"EXP77-1360","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"Landscape with the Flight into Egypt","value":"Annibale Carracci","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landscape_with_the_Flight_into_Egypt"}],"explanation74":"Landscape with the Flight into Egypt is associated with Annibale Carracci. Ideal landscape makes the biblical narrative part of a wider natural order."},{"id":"EXP77-1361","nodeId":"EXP77-1361","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Martyrdom of Saint Matthew","value":"Caravaggio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Martyrdom_of_Saint_Matthew"}],"explanation74":"Martyrdom of Saint Matthew is associated with Caravaggio. Companion to the Calling in the Contarelli Chapel."},{"id":"EXP77-1362","nodeId":"EXP77-1362","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q046","subject":"De remediis utriusque fortunae","value":"Petrarch","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_remediis_utriusque_fortunae"}],"explanation74":"De remediis utriusque fortunae is associated with Petrarch. Dialogues addressing good and adverse fortune."},{"id":"EXP77-1363","nodeId":"EXP77-1363","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q238","subject":"Six Books of the Commonwealth","value":"Jean Bodin","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Les_Six_Livres_de_la_R%C3%A9publique"}],"explanation74":"Six Books of the Commonwealth is associated with Jean Bodin. French treatise on sovereignty; not Machiavelli's Prince."},{"id":"EXP77-1364","nodeId":"EXP77-1364","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Gulliver's Travels","value":"Jonathan Swift","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulliver's_Travels"}],"explanation74":"Gulliver's Travels is associated with Jonathan Swift. Satire of politics, society and human reason."},{"id":"EXP77-1365","nodeId":"EXP77-1365","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q238","subject":"On the Law of War and Peace","value":"Hugo Grotius","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_jure_belli_ac_pacis"}],"explanation74":"On the Law of War and Peace is associated with Hugo Grotius. Foundational work in the history of international law."},{"id":"EXP77-1366","nodeId":"EXP77-1366","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q486","subject":"Cellini Salt Cellar","value":"Benvenuto Cellini","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cellini_Salt_Cellar"}],"explanation74":"Cellini Salt Cellar is associated with Benvenuto Cellini. Gold and enamel table sculpture for Francis I."},{"id":"EXP77-1367","nodeId":"EXP77-1367","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"The Fortune Teller","value":"Caravaggio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Fortune_Teller_(Caravaggio)"}],"explanation74":"The Fortune Teller is associated with Caravaggio. Two versions; source does not specify which, so date is a broad range."},{"id":"EXP77-1368","nodeId":"EXP77-1368","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q203","subject":"Bridge at Narni","value":"Camille Corot","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Contemporary","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bridge_at_Narni"}],"explanation74":"Bridge at Narni is associated with Camille Corot. Outdoor oil study; distinguish the larger Salon composition."},{"id":"EXP77-1369","nodeId":"EXP77-1369","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q294","subject":"Eleonora of Toledo and Her Son","value":"Bronzino","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Eleanor_of_Toledo"}],"explanation74":"Eleonora of Toledo and Her Son is associated with Bronzino. Medici dynastic portrait; textile detail is politically significant."},{"id":"EXP77-1370","nodeId":"EXP77-1370","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q631","subject":"Peasant Wedding","value":"Pieter Bruegel the Elder","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Peasant_Wedding"}],"explanation74":"Peasant Wedding is associated with Pieter Bruegel the Elder. Village banquet; social observation rather than courtly portraiture."},{"id":"EXP77-1371","nodeId":"EXP77-1371","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Psyche Revived by Cupid's Kiss","value":"Antonio Canova","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psyche_Revived_by_Cupid's_Kiss"}],"explanation74":"Psyche Revived by Cupid's Kiss is associated with Antonio Canova. First marble version, now at the Louvre."},{"id":"EXP77-1372","nodeId":"EXP77-1372","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q003","subject":"Pierrot, formerly called Gilles","value":"Antoine Watteau","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierrot_(Watteau)"}],"explanation74":"Pierrot, formerly called Gilles is associated with Antoine Watteau. Louvre painting; identity and theatrical context remain debated."},{"id":"EXP77-1373","nodeId":"EXP77-1373","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q050","subject":"Letters Concerning the English Nation","value":"Voltaire","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Letters_on_the_English"}],"explanation74":"Letters Concerning the English Nation is associated with Voltaire. French publication; English edition had appeared in 1733."},{"id":"EXP77-1374","nodeId":"EXP77-1374","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q294","subject":"Diana Leaving Her Bath","value":"François Boucher","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diana_Leaving_her_Bath"}],"explanation74":"Diana Leaving Her Bath is associated with François Boucher. Mythology treated through Rococo intimacy."},{"id":"EXP77-1375","nodeId":"EXP77-1375","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Transavanguardia named","value":"Achille Bonito Oliva","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transavantgarde"}],"explanation74":"Transavanguardia named is associated with Achille Bonito Oliva. An Italian critical label associated with a return to expressive painting."},{"id":"EXP77-1376","nodeId":"EXP77-1376","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q225","subject":"Inquiry into the Principles of Political Oeconomy","value":"James Steuart","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.otaru-uc.ac.jp/htosyo1/siryo/yosho/Y_steuart.htm"}],"explanation74":"Inquiry into the Principles of Political Oeconomy is associated with James Steuart. Two-volume treatise. Corrects the ambiguous Stuart/Steuart annotation in the photo."},{"id":"EXP77-1377","nodeId":"EXP77-1377","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q046","subject":"De viris illustribus","value":"Petrarch","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Viris_Illustribus_(Petrarch)"}],"explanation74":"De viris illustribus is associated with Petrarch. Biographical collection revised over much of Petrarch's life."},{"id":"EXP77-1378","nodeId":"EXP77-1378","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q059","subject":"Sidereus Nuncius","value":"Galileo Galilei","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sidereus_Nuncius"}],"explanation74":"Sidereus Nuncius is associated with Galileo Galilei. Telescopic observations, including Jupiter's satellites."},{"id":"EXP77-1379","nodeId":"EXP77-1379","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q087","subject":"The Miser","value":"Molière","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Miser"}],"explanation74":"The Miser is associated with Molière. Comedy first performed in Paris."},{"id":"EXP77-1380","nodeId":"EXP77-1380","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q150","subject":"Palazzo Medici Riccardi","value":"Michelozzo","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palazzo_Medici_Riccardi"}],"explanation74":"Palazzo Medici Riccardi is associated with Michelozzo. Michelozzo's palace for Cosimo de' Medici; later extended by the Riccardi."},{"id":"EXP77-1381","nodeId":"EXP77-1381","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q009","subject":"Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum opens","value":"Frank Lloyd Wright","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solomon_R._Guggenheim_Museum"}],"explanation74":"Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum opens is associated with Frank Lloyd Wright. The New York museum's spiral ramp organises a continuous exhibition route."},{"id":"EXP77-1382","nodeId":"EXP77-1382","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q128","subject":"Battle of San Romano","value":"Paolo Uccello","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/paolo-uccello-the-battle-of-san-romano"}],"explanation74":"Battle of San Romano is associated with Paolo Uccello. Three panels depict the battle of 1432; painting date is later and debated."},{"id":"EXP77-1383","nodeId":"EXP77-1383","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Procession of the Magi","value":"Benozzo Gozzoli","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magi_Chapel"}],"explanation74":"Procession of the Magi is associated with Benozzo Gozzoli. Medici palace chapel; includes portraits within the biblical procession."},{"id":"EXP77-1384","nodeId":"EXP77-1384","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q128","subject":"Crucifixion, Scuola Grande di San Rocco","value":"Tintoretto","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crucifixion_(Tintoretto)"}],"explanation74":"Crucifixion, Scuola Grande di San Rocco is associated with Tintoretto. Large narrative composition within Tintoretto's Venetian cycle."},{"id":"EXP77-1385","nodeId":"EXP77-1385","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q560","subject":"Saint George and the Dragon","value":"Paolo Uccello","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_George_and_the_Dragon_(Uccello)"}],"explanation74":"Saint George and the Dragon is associated with Paolo Uccello. London version used as the reference example; other versions exist."},{"id":"EXP77-1386","nodeId":"EXP77-1386","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Martyrdom of Saint Philip","value":"Jusepe de Ribera","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Martyrdom_of_Saint_Philip"}],"explanation74":"Martyrdom of Saint Philip is associated with Jusepe de Ribera. Formerly interpreted as Saint Bartholomew; subject corrected."},{"id":"EXP77-1387","nodeId":"EXP77-1387","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q321","subject":"Pilgrimage to Cythera","value":"Antoine Watteau","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Embarkation_for_Cythera"}],"explanation74":"Pilgrimage to Cythera is associated with Antoine Watteau. Louvre reception piece; distinguish the later Berlin version."},{"id":"EXP77-1388","nodeId":"EXP77-1388","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q203","subject":"Charles I at the Hunt","value":"Anthony van Dyck","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Contemporary","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_I_at_the_Hunt"}],"explanation74":"Charles I at the Hunt is associated with Anthony van Dyck. Royal authority presented through apparently informal dress and setting."},{"id":"EXP77-1389","nodeId":"EXP77-1389","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q152","subject":"A Rake's Progress","value":"William Hogarth","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://collections.soane.org/object-p41"}],"explanation74":"A Rake's Progress is associated with William Hogarth. Eight paintings; engravings published in 1735."},{"id":"EXP77-1390","nodeId":"EXP77-1390","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Robinson Crusoe","value":"Daniel Defoe","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robinson_Crusoe"}],"explanation74":"Robinson Crusoe is associated with Daniel Defoe. First publication of the novel."},{"id":"EXP77-1391","nodeId":"EXP77-1391","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q290","subject":"Ognissanti Madonna","value":"Giotto","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Medieval","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ognissanti_Madonna"}],"explanation74":"Ognissanti Madonna is associated with Giotto. Tempera and gold on panel; volumetric figures within a Gothic throne."},{"id":"EXP77-1392","nodeId":"EXP77-1392","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"The Garden of Love","value":"Peter Paul Rubens","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://www.museodelprado.es/en/the-collection/art-work/the-garden-of-love/8c9e39be-c70c-403a-a386-e7609e7af236"}],"explanation74":"The Garden of Love is associated with Peter Paul Rubens. Prado painting; courtly couples, putti and a garden setting."},{"id":"EXP77-1393","nodeId":"EXP77-1393","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q294","subject":"Portrait of Charles VII","value":"Jean Fouquet","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_Charles_VII_of_France"}],"explanation74":"Portrait of Charles VII is associated with Jean Fouquet. Royal portrait now at the Louvre; approximate dating."},{"id":"EXP77-1394","nodeId":"EXP77-1394","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q128","subject":"San Zaccaria Altarpiece","value":"Giovanni Bellini","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Zaccaria_Altarpiece"}],"explanation74":"San Zaccaria Altarpiece is associated with Giovanni Bellini. Sacra conversazione set within a unified architectural space."},{"id":"EXP77-1395","nodeId":"EXP77-1395","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"The Joy of Life","value":"Henri Matisse","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Bonheur_de_vivre"}],"explanation74":"The Joy of Life is associated with Henri Matisse. Also called Le Bonheur de vivre. Fauvist colour and an idealised figure landscape; the handwritten placement is not a precise creation date."},{"id":"EXP77-1396","nodeId":"EXP77-1396","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q637","subject":"Melun Diptych","value":"Jean Fouquet","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melun_Diptych"}],"explanation74":"Melun Diptych is associated with Jean Fouquet. Étienne Chevalier and Saint Stephen paired with the Virgin and Child."},{"id":"EXP77-1397","nodeId":"EXP77-1397","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q068","subject":"Uffizi palace","value":"Giorgio Vasari","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"19th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uffizi"}],"explanation74":"Uffizi palace is associated with Giorgio Vasari. Built as administrative offices for Cosimo I; completed after Vasari's death."},{"id":"EXP77-1398","nodeId":"EXP77-1398","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"Apollo and the Continents","value":"Giambattista Tiepolo","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W%C3%BCrzburg_Residence"}],"explanation74":"Apollo and the Continents is associated with Giambattista Tiepolo. Grand staircase fresco in Neumann's Würzburg Residence."},{"id":"EXP77-1399","nodeId":"EXP77-1399","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q199","subject":"Abduction of a Sabine Woman","value":"Giambologna","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"20th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abduction_of_a_Sabine_Woman"}],"explanation74":"Abduction of a Sabine Woman is associated with Giambologna. Giambologna; spiralling three-figure marble group."},{"id":"EXP77-1401","nodeId":"EXP77-1401","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q741","subject":"Faust, Part One","value":"Johann Wolfgang von Goethe","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goethe's_Faust"}],"explanation74":"Faust, Part One is associated with Johann Wolfgang von Goethe. Part One publication; Part Two published posthumously in 1832."},{"id":"EXP77-1402","nodeId":"EXP77-1402","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Equestrian statue of Gattamelata","value":"Donatello","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equestrian_statue_of_Gattamelata"}],"explanation74":"Equestrian statue of Gattamelata is associated with Donatello. Padua; bronze monument to the condottiere Erasmo da Narni."},{"id":"EXP77-1403","nodeId":"EXP77-1403","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Fall of the Giants","value":"Giulio Romano","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sala_dei_Giganti"}],"explanation74":"Fall of the Giants is associated with Giulio Romano. Palazzo Te; painted architecture dissolves the room's boundaries."},{"id":"EXP77-1404","nodeId":"EXP77-1404","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"The Valpinçon Bather","value":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Valpin%C3%A7on_Bather"}],"explanation74":"The Valpinçon Bather is associated with Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres. Figure seen from behind; an important precursor to The Turkish Bath."},{"id":"EXP77-1405","nodeId":"EXP77-1405","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q031","subject":"Sarah Siddons as the Tragic Muse","value":"Joshua Reynolds","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarah_Siddons_as_the_Tragic_Muse"}],"explanation74":"Sarah Siddons as the Tragic Muse is associated with Joshua Reynolds. Portrait identifying the actor with Melpomene; Huntington version."},{"id":"EXP77-1406","nodeId":"EXP77-1406","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q046","subject":"De pictura","value":"Leon Battista Alberti","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_pictura"}],"explanation74":"De pictura is associated with Leon Battista Alberti. Latin treatise on painting; Italian version followed in 1436."},{"id":"EXP77-1407","nodeId":"EXP77-1407","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"Grande Odalisque","value":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grande_Odalisque"}],"explanation74":"Grande Odalisque is associated with Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres. Orientalist subject and deliberately elongated anatomy."},{"id":"EXP77-1408","nodeId":"EXP77-1408","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q705","subject":"Las Hilanderas","value":"Diego Velázquez","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early 20th century","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Las_Hilanderas"}],"explanation74":"Las Hilanderas is associated with Diego Velázquez. Myth of Arachne represented through a tapestry workshop."},{"id":"EXP77-1409","nodeId":"EXP77-1409","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q066","subject":"Blind Leading the Blind","value":"Pieter Bruegel the Elder","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blind_Leading_the_Blind"}],"explanation74":"Blind Leading the Blind is associated with Pieter Bruegel the Elder. Biblical parable rendered as a sequence of falling figures."},{"id":"EXP77-1410","nodeId":"EXP77-1410","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q035","subject":"On the Infinite, Universe and Worlds","value":"Giordano Bruno","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giordano_Bruno"}],"explanation74":"On the Infinite, Universe and Worlds is associated with Giordano Bruno. Italian dialogue; publication in London."},{"id":"EXP77-1411","nodeId":"EXP77-1411","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q673","subject":"Venus, Cupid, Folly and Time","value":"Bronzino","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus,_Cupid,_Folly_and_Time"}],"explanation74":"Venus, Cupid, Folly and Time is associated with Bronzino. Allegory with deliberately complex iconography."},{"id":"EXP77-1412","nodeId":"EXP77-1412","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Well of Moses","value":"Claus Sluter","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Well_of_Moses"}],"explanation74":"Well of Moses is associated with Claus Sluter. Chartreuse de Champmol; completed with workshop participation."},{"id":"EXP77-1413","nodeId":"EXP77-1413","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q054","subject":"Charles V at Mühlberg","value":"Titian","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equestrian_Portrait_of_Charles_V"}],"explanation74":"Charles V at Mühlberg is associated with Titian. Commemorates the emperor's victory of 1547."},{"id":"EXP77-1414","nodeId":"EXP77-1414","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"The Sower","value":"Jean-François Millet","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sower_(Millet)"}],"explanation74":"The Sower is associated with Jean-François Millet. Boston painting as a reference example; later variants and prints also exist."},{"id":"EXP77-1415","nodeId":"EXP77-1415","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q132","subject":"David, bronze","value":"Donatello","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Donatello)"}],"explanation74":"David, bronze is associated with Donatello. Approximate, debated date; distinguish from Donatello's earlier marble David."},{"id":"EXP77-1416","nodeId":"EXP77-1416","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q718","subject":"Romeo and Juliet","value":"William Shakespeare","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romeo_and_Juliet"}],"explanation74":"Romeo and Juliet is associated with William Shakespeare. Approximate composition; first quarto printed 1597."},{"id":"EXP77-1417","nodeId":"EXP77-1417","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q158","subject":"Royal Saltworks at Arc-et-Senans","value":"Claude-Nicolas Ledoux","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Saltworks_at_Arc-et-Senans"}],"explanation74":"Royal Saltworks at Arc-et-Senans is associated with Claude-Nicolas Ledoux. Industrial architecture; distinguish the built saltworks from the ideal city of Chaux."},{"id":"EXP77-1418","nodeId":"EXP77-1418","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Disputation of the Holy Sacrament","value":"Raphael","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disputation_of_the_Holy_Sacrament"}],"explanation74":"Disputation of the Holy Sacrament is associated with Raphael. Theology fresco opposite the School of Athens."},{"id":"EXP77-1419","nodeId":"EXP77-1419","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q631","subject":"Hunters in the Snow","value":"Pieter Bruegel the Elder","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Hunters_in_the_Snow"}],"explanation74":"Hunters in the Snow is associated with Pieter Bruegel the Elder. Winter panel from a cycle of the seasons."},{"id":"EXP77-1420","nodeId":"EXP77-1420","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q087","subject":"The Robbers","value":"Friedrich Schiller","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Robbers"}],"explanation74":"The Robbers is associated with Friedrich Schiller. Published 1781; first staged in 1782."},{"id":"EXP77-1421","nodeId":"EXP77-1421","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Immaculate Conception of Los Venerables","value":"Bartolomé Esteban Murillo","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immaculate_Conception_of_Los_Venerables"}],"explanation74":"Immaculate Conception of Los Venerables is associated with Bartolomé Esteban Murillo. Distinct from Murillo's many other versions of the subject."},{"id":"EXP77-1422","nodeId":"EXP77-1422","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q238","subject":"History of the Art of Antiquity","value":"Johann Joachim Winckelmann","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann_Joachim_Winckelmann"}],"explanation74":"History of the Art of Antiquity is associated with Johann Joachim Winckelmann. Influential account of ancient art and stylistic development."},{"id":"EXP77-1423","nodeId":"EXP77-1423","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"David with the Head of Goliath","value":"Caravaggio","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_with_the_Head_of_Goliath_(Caravaggio,_Rome)"}],"explanation74":"David with the Head of Goliath is associated with Caravaggio. Borghese version; dates debated. Other versions have different chronologies."},{"id":"EXP77-1424","nodeId":"EXP77-1424","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"Landscape with Saint John on Patmos","value":"Nicolas Poussin","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landscape_with_Saint_John_on_Patmos"}],"explanation74":"Landscape with Saint John on Patmos is associated with Nicolas Poussin. Classical ruins and ideal landscape frame the sacred subject."},{"id":"EXP77-1425","nodeId":"EXP77-1425","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q073","subject":"The Ancient of Days","value":"William Blake","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ancient_of_Days"}],"explanation74":"The Ancient of Days is associated with William Blake. Design for Europe a Prophecy; later impressions also exist."},{"id":"EXP77-1426","nodeId":"EXP77-1426","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q064","subject":"Two Treatises of Government","value":"John Locke","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two_Treatises_of_Government"}],"explanation74":"Two Treatises of Government is associated with John Locke. Published in 1689 with a title-page date of 1690."},{"id":"EXP77-1427","nodeId":"EXP77-1427","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q238","subject":"On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres","value":"Nicolaus Copernicus","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Renaissance","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_revolutionibus_orbium_coelestium"}],"explanation74":"On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres is associated with Nicolaus Copernicus. Heliocentric astronomical treatise; published in Nuremberg."},{"id":"EXP77-1428","nodeId":"EXP77-1428","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q604","subject":"Summa Theologiae","value":"Thomas Aquinas","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Medieval","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summa_Theologica"}],"explanation74":"Summa Theologiae is associated with Thomas Aquinas. Thomas Aquinas's unfinished theological synthesis."},{"id":"EXP77-1429","nodeId":"EXP77-1429","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q702","subject":"Napoleon I on His Imperial Throne","value":"Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon_I_on_His_Imperial_Throne"}],"explanation74":"Napoleon I on His Imperial Throne is associated with Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres. Imperial portrait invoking sacred and historical models of power."},{"id":"EXP77-1430","nodeId":"EXP77-1430","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q012","subject":"Canterbury Tales","value":"Geoffrey Chaucer","group":"attribution","major":"Language & Literature","era":"Early modern","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Canterbury_Tales"}],"explanation74":"Canterbury Tales is associated with Geoffrey Chaucer. Unfinished collection framed by a pilgrimage."},{"id":"EXP77-1431","nodeId":"EXP77-1431","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q759","subject":"Four Apostles","value":"Albrecht Dürer","group":"attribution","major":"Art History","era":"Ancient","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Four_Apostles"}],"explanation74":"Four Apostles is associated with Albrecht Dürer. Panels presented to the city of Nuremberg."},{"id":"EXP77-1432","nodeId":"EXP77-1432","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q735","subject":"First Dresden Court Theatre","value":"Gottfried Semper","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semperoper"}],"explanation74":"First Dresden Court Theatre is associated with Gottfried Semper. Original theatre; destroyed in 1869, later rebuilt to a different design."},{"id":"EXP77-1433","nodeId":"EXP77-1433","anchor":"ALPHA-B006-Q613","subject":"Sydney Opera House opens","value":"Jørn Utzon","group":"attribution","major":"Architecture","era":"Ancient","relation":"attribution","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sydney_Opera_House"}],"explanation74":"Sydney Opera House opens is associated with Jørn Utzon. Opened after a long construction process; Utzon left the project in 1966."},{"id":"EXP77-1434","nodeId":"EXP77-1434","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q494","subject":"The Peace of Amiens briefly ended hostilities between France and which power?","value":"Britain","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Amiens"}],"explanation74":"The 1802 peace temporarily ended the war between Britain and France; fighting resumed in 1803."},{"id":"EXP77-1435","nodeId":"EXP77-1435","anchor":"ALPHA-B005-Q451","subject":"The League of Armed Neutrality promoted by Catherine II sought to protect what?","value":"Neutral shipping","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_League_of_Armed_Neutrality"}],"explanation74":"The league defended the maritime trading rights of neutral powers during the American Revolutionary War."},{"id":"EXP77-1436","nodeId":"EXP77-1436","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"Louis IX died near Tunis during which expedition?","value":"Eighth Crusade","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eighth_Crusade"}],"explanation74":"Louis IX's 1270 expedition to Tunis is known as the Eighth Crusade."},{"id":"EXP77-1437","nodeId":"EXP77-1437","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q480","subject":"The English East India Company originally operated under which legal instrument?","value":"Royal charter","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Ancient","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_India_Company"}],"explanation74":"Elizabeth I granted the company a royal charter in 1600, authorising its trading monopoly."},{"id":"EXP77-1438","nodeId":"EXP77-1438","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q023","subject":"Wat Tyler is associated with which English revolt?","value":"Peasants' Revolt","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peasants'_Revolt"}],"explanation74":"Wat Tyler was a leader of the Peasants' Revolt of 1381, which challenged taxation and serfdom."},{"id":"EXP77-1439","nodeId":"EXP77-1439","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q126","subject":"Rome destroyed Corinth in the same year as which other ancient city?","value":"Carthage","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Medieval","relation":"chronology comparison","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Corinth_(146_BC)"}],"explanation74":"Both Corinth and Carthage were destroyed by Roman armies in 146 BCE."},{"id":"EXP77-1440","nodeId":"EXP77-1440","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q023","subject":"Which defeated power rejoined the European concert at Aix-la-Chapelle in 1818?","value":"France","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congress_of_Aix-la-Chapelle_(1818)"}],"explanation74":"The congress ended the Allied occupation of France and admitted it to the concert of major European powers."},{"id":"EXP77-1441","nodeId":"EXP77-1441","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q013","subject":"The Greek War of Independence sought separation from which empire?","value":"Ottoman Empire","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Protocol_(1830)"}],"explanation74":"Greek independence from Ottoman rule was internationally recognised in 1830."},{"id":"EXP77-1442","nodeId":"EXP77-1442","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q083","subject":"European Union established","value":"1993","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"history date","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/collection/eu-law/treaties/treaties-founding.html?locale=en"}],"explanation74":"European Union established: 1993. The Maastricht Treaty entered into force on 1 November."},{"id":"EXP77-1443","nodeId":"EXP77-1443","anchor":"ALPHA-B004-Q308","subject":"Foucault's pendulum provides a demonstration of what?","value":"Earth's rotation","group":"history date","major":"Science & Tech","era":"Renaissance","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foucault_pendulum"}],"explanation74":"The changing orientation of the swing relative to the ground demonstrates Earth's rotation."},{"id":"EXP77-1444","nodeId":"EXP77-1444","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q022","subject":"Napoleon met Alexander I on a raft at Tilsit. Alexander ruled which country?","value":"Russia","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaties_of_Tilsit"}],"explanation74":"The 1807 settlement followed Napoleon's victories; Alexander I represented Russia."},{"id":"EXP77-1445","nodeId":"EXP77-1445","anchor":"ALPHA-B008-Q770","subject":"The Union of Utrecht brought together provinces in which region?","value":"Northern Netherlands","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Late 20th century","relation":"place geography","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union_of_Utrecht"}],"explanation74":"The 1579 union linked the northern provinces during the revolt against Spanish rule."},{"id":"EXP77-1446","nodeId":"EXP77-1446","anchor":"ALPHA-B003-Q197","subject":"The abbreviation VOC refers to which trading organisation?","value":"Dutch East India Company","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"term function","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_East_India_Company"}],"explanation74":"VOC abbreviates Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie, founded in the Netherlands in 1602."},{"id":"EXP77-1447","nodeId":"EXP77-1447","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q024","subject":"The German Empire was proclaimed in 1871 in which palace?","value":"Versailles","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"19th century","relation":"place geography","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proclamation_of_the_German_Empire"}],"explanation74":"William I was proclaimed German emperor in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles during the Franco-Prussian War."},{"id":"EXP77-1448","nodeId":"EXP77-1448","anchor":"ALPHA-B007-Q517","subject":"Thomas More's refusal to accept royal supremacy brought him into conflict with which monarch?","value":"Henry VIII","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_More"}],"explanation74":"More refused to recognise Henry VIII as supreme head of the English Church and was executed in 1535."},{"id":"EXP77-1449","nodeId":"EXP77-1449","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q023","subject":"On which island did Napoleon spend his final exile?","value":"Saint Helena","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"place geography","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napoleon"}],"explanation74":"Napoleon was first exiled to Elba. After Waterloo, he was sent to Saint Helena, where he died in 1821."},{"id":"EXP77-1450","nodeId":"EXP77-1450","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q095","subject":"The capture of John II at Poitiers occurred during which conflict?","value":"Hundred Years' War","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Poitiers"}],"explanation74":"The English victory at Poitiers in 1356 was a major episode in the Hundred Years' War."},{"id":"EXP77-1451","nodeId":"EXP77-1451","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q040","subject":"Treaty of Tordesillas","value":"1494","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"history date","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Tordesillas"}],"explanation74":"Treaty of Tordesillas: 1494. Iberian division of overseas claims."},{"id":"EXP77-1452","nodeId":"EXP77-1452","anchor":"ALPHA-B002-Q095","subject":"At Cannae, Hannibal defeated an army belonging to which state?","value":"Roman Republic","group":"history date","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern","relation":"context relation","references":[{"file":"Tapestry source record","page":null,"verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Cannae"}],"explanation74":"Hannibal's Carthaginian army defeated Roman forces at Cannae during the Second Punic War."},{"id":"EXP77-1453","nodeId":"EXP77-1453","anchor":"ALPHA-B001-Q022","subject":"Relief of Orléans","value":"1429","group":"history date","major":"History & Poli